                  "section_title": "5. Real-time & Events Architecture"
                },
                "security_hardening_plan": {
                  "content": "A 'security-first' approach will be adopted, addressing OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities:\n\n*   **Input Validation:** Validate all user inputs on both the client and server-side to prevent injection attacks (SQL injection, XSS). Use Laravel's built-in validation features and sanitize data.\n*   **Output Encoding:** Encode all output data to prevent XSS attacks.\n*   **Authentication & Authorization:** Implement strong authentication and authorization mechanisms using Laravel Sanctum and Policies. Use multi-factor authentication where possible.\n*   **Password Hashing:** Use bcrypt or Argon2 to hash passwords.\n*   **Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) Protection:** Use Laravel's CSRF protection.\n*   **Security Headers:** Implement security headers (e.g., `Content-Security-Policy`, `X-Frame-Options`, `X-XSS-Protection`) to mitigate various attacks.\n*   **Regular Security Audits:** Conduct regular security audits and penetration testing to identify and fix vulnerabilities. Tools like SonarQube could be utilized.\n*   **Dependency Management:** Keep all dependencies up-to-date to patch known vulnerabilities.\n*   **Rate Limiting:** Implement rate limiting to prevent brute-force attacks.\n*   **Error Handling:** Implement proper error handling and logging to prevent information leakage. Avoid displaying sensitive information in error messages.\n*   **Secure File Uploads:** Implement secure file upload mechanisms to prevent malicious file uploads. Use a Content Delivery Network (CDN) like Amazon CloudFront or Cloudflare to store and serve static content, including user-uploaded files [https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/].\n\nOWASP ZAP [https://owasp.org/www-project-zap/] will be used for penetration testing. Snyk [https://snyk.io/] will be used for static code analysis and vulnerability scanning.\n",
