        "architecture": {
          "document_exists": true,
          "raw_content": "```markdown\n# subtrack-ai Architecture\n\n## Overview\n\nAI-powered subscription manager. Automatically tracks, optimizes, and negotiates better rates for your subscriptions. Save money effortlessly!\n\n### Problem Statement\n\nIndividuals waste money on forgotten, unused, or overpriced subscriptions. Managing them manually is tedious and ineffective. Users often lose track of their subscriptions, leading to unnecessary costs and missed opportunities to negotiate better deals. Existing solutions are often complex, requiring significant manual effort and expertise.\n\n### Target Audience\n\nIndividuals with 3+ subscriptions (streaming, SaaS, memberships) who want to save money and simplify their financial lives. This includes tech-savvy individuals, busy professionals, and families looking to streamline their finances. The target audience is comfortable with technology and appreciates the convenience of automated solutions. They are motivated by saving money and simplifying subscription management.\n\n### Core Entities\n\n*   **User:** Represents an individual user of the system. Contains information such as name, email, password, payment method, and subscription preferences.\n*   **Subscription:** Represents a subscription service the user is signed up for. Contains information such as service name, billing cycle, price, renewal date, payment method, and status (active, inactive, trial).\n*   **Transaction:** Records payments made for subscriptions. Contains information such as date, amount, payment method, and subscription ID.\n*   **Provider:** Represents the subscription service provider (e.g., Netflix, Spotify, Adobe). Contains information such as name, contact information, and supported subscription plans.\n*   **Recommendation:** AI-generated suggestions for optimizing subscriptions. Contains information such as potential savings, alternative plans, and negotiation strategies.\n*   **Negotiation:** Records the process and outcome of negotiating better rates with subscription providers. Contains information such as negotiation date, agent involved (if applicable), original price, new price, and outcome.\n*   **Alert:** Notifications triggered based on subscription events (e.g., upcoming renewal, price increase, potential savings). Contains information such as message, urgency, and associated subscription.\n*   **Role:** Represents the role of a user, determining access permissions (e.g., Admin, User).\n*   **Permission:** Defines specific actions a user can perform within the system (e.g., create subscription, view reports).\n\n## Design System\n\n### Typography\n\n*   **Font**: Inter\n*   **Font Stack**: `'Inter', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Fira Sans', 'Droid Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif`\n*   **Google Fonts**: [https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Inter](https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Inter)\n*   **Rationale**: Inter is a modern, highly readable font optimized for screens. Its variable font weights allow for fine-grained control over typography, ensuring a polished and professional look. Its open-source nature and wide browser support make it a practical choice.\n\n### Color Palette\n\nThis color palette is designed to provide a professional and accessible visual experience. The colors have been chosen to ensure sufficient contrast for readability, adhering to WCAG AA guidelines. The logical structure makes it easy to maintain and extend the palette as needed.\n\n*   **Indigo**: `#4F46E5` - Primary actions, main branding color. Used for buttons, links, and key UI elements.\n*   **Slate Gray**: `#64748B` - Secondary text, less important UI elements. Used for labels, muted text, and subtle backgrounds.\n*   **Emerald Green**: `#10B981` - Call-to-action, highlights, success states. Used sparingly to draw attention to important elements.\n*   **Charcoal Gray**: `#1E293B` - Primary text color for high readability.\n*   **Off-White**: `#F8FAFC` - Main background color for a clean and modern look.\n*   **Light Gray**: `#E2E8F0` - Borders and dividers to create visual separation.\n*   **Teal**: `#14B8A6` - Success messages and confirmations.\n*   **Amber**: `#F59E0B` - Warning messages and alerts.\n*   **Rose Red**: `#E11D48` - Error messages and critical alerts.\n\n## System Architecture\n\nThe system follows a microservices architecture to ensure scalability, maintainability, and fault tolerance. The core components include:\n\n*   **Frontend:** User interface built with React, providing both user and admin portals.\n*   **API Gateway:** Entry point for all requests, routing them to the appropriate microservices. Handles authentication, authorization, and rate limiting.\n*   **User Service:** Manages user accounts, profiles, and authentication.\n*   **Subscription Service:** Manages subscription data, including tracking renewal dates, prices, and status.\n*   **Transaction Service:** Processes and records subscription payments.\n*   **Recommendation Engine:** AI-powered service that analyzes subscription data and provides recommendations for optimization and negotiation.\n*   **Negotiation Service:** Automates the negotiation process with subscription providers.\n*   **Alerting Service:** Generates and sends alerts to users based on subscription events.\n*   **Admin Service:** Provides administrative tools for managing users, providers, and system configuration.\n*   **Data Ingestion Service:** Ingests data from various sources like bank statements, email inboxes, and provider APIs to identify subscriptions.\n\n**Admin/User Separation:**\n\n*   The system has distinct portals for administrators and regular users.\n*   The **Admin Portal** provides access to system-wide settings, user management, provider management, and reporting.\n*   The **User Portal** allows users to manage their subscriptions, view recommendations, track spending, and initiate negotiations.\n*   Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) ensures that users only have access to the features and data they are authorized to view and modify.\n\n## Database Schema\n\nThe system uses a relational database (e.g., PostgreSQL) to store persistent data.\n\n**Core Tables:**\n\n*   **users:** (id, email, password, first_name, last_name, created_at, updated_at)\n*   **subscriptions:** (id, user_id, provider_id, name, billing_cycle, price, renewal_date, payment_method, status, created_at, updated_at)\n*   **transactions:** (id, subscription_id, date, amount, payment_method, created_at, updated_at)\n*   **providers:** (id, name, contact_information, api_endpoint, created_at, updated_at)\n*   **recommendations:** (id, user_id, subscription_id, potential_savings, alternative_plan, negotiation_strategy, created_at, updated_at)\n*   **negotiations:** (id, subscription_id, negotiation_date, agent_involved, original_price, new_price, outcome, created_at, updated_at)\n*   **alerts:** (id, user_id, subscription_id, message, urgency, created_at, updated_at)\n\n**RBAC Tables:**\n\n*   **roles:** (id, name, description)\n*   **permissions:** (id, name, description)\n*   **role_permissions:** (role_id, permission_id)\n*   **user_roles:** (user_id, role_id)\n\n**Relationships:**\n\n*   One-to-many: `users` to `subscriptions`, `subscriptions` to `transactions`, `users` to `alerts`\n*   Many-to-many: `users` to `roles`, `roles` to `permissions`\n\n## API Design\n\nThe system exposes a RESTful API for both users and administrators. The API uses JSON for data exchange and supports authentication via JWT (JSON Web Tokens).\n\n**Admin Endpoints (Examples):**\n\n*   `GET /admin/users`: List all users (paginated).\n*   `GET /admin/users/{id}`: Get user by ID.\n*   `POST /admin/users`: Create a new user.\n*   `PUT /admin/users/{id}`: Update user details.\n*   `DELETE /admin/users/{id}`: Delete a user.\n*   `GET /admin/providers`: List all providers.\n*   `POST /admin/providers`: Create a new provider.\n*   `PUT /admin/providers/{id}`: Update provider details.\n*   `DELETE /admin/providers/{id}`: Delete a provider.\n*   `GET /admin/subscriptions`: List all subscriptions (across all users).\n*   `GET /admin/reports/spending`: Generate spending reports.\n*   `GET /admin/reports/negotiations`: Generate negotiation reports.\n*   `GET /admin/roles`: List all roles.\n*   `POST /admin/roles`: Create a new role.\n*   `PUT /admin/roles/{id}`: Update a role.\n*   `DELETE /admin/roles/{id}`: Delete a role.\n*   `GET /admin/permissions`: List all permissions.\n*   `POST /admin/permissions`: Create a new permission.\n*   `GET /admin/system/settings`: Retrieves system settings.\n*   `PUT /admin/system/settings`: Updates system settings.\n*   `GET /admin/logs`: Retrieves application logs.\n*   `GET /admin/audit`: Retrieves audit logs.\n*   `POST /admin/trigger_ai_analysis`: Triggers AI analysis for all users.\n*   `GET /admin/dashboard`: Retrieves key admin dashboard statistics.\n\n**User Endpoints (Examples):**\n\n*   `GET /users/me`: Get current user profile.\n*   `PUT /users/me`: Update current user profile.\n*   `GET /subscriptions`: List user's subscriptions.\n*   `POST /subscriptions`: Add a new subscription.\n*   `GET /subscriptions/{id}`: Get subscription by ID.\n*   `PUT /subscriptions/{id}`: Update subscription details.\n*   `DELETE /subscriptions/{id}`: Delete a subscription.\n*   `GET /transactions`: List user's transactions.\n*   `GET /recommendations`: List user's recommendations.\n*   `POST /negotiations`: Initiate a negotiation for a subscription.\n*   `GET /negotiations/{id}`: Get negotiation details.\n*   `GET /alerts`: List user's alerts.\n*   `POST /auth/register`: Register a new user.\n*   `POST /auth/login`: Login a user.\n*   `POST /auth/reset-password`: Request a password reset.\n*   `PUT /auth/reset-password/{token}`: Reset user password.\n*   `GET /billing/cards`: List user's saved payment cards.\n*   `POST /billing/cards`: Add a new payment card.\n*   `DELETE /billing/cards/{id}`: Delete a payment card.\n*   `GET /profile`: retrieves the user's profile\n*   `PUT /profile`: updates the user's profile\n*   `GET /providers`: retrieves the list of subscription providers\n\n## Frontend Structure\n\nThe frontend is built using React and is structured into two main portals:\n\n*   **User Portal:**\n    *   `src/user`: Contains the code for the user-facing application.\n    *   `src/user/components`: Reusable UI components.\n    *   `src/user/pages`: Individual pages for managing subscriptions, viewing recommendations, tracking spending, etc.\n    *   `src/user/services`: API client for interacting with the backend API.\n*   **Admin Portal:**\n    *   `src/admin`: Contains the code for the admin application.\n    *   `src/admin/components`: Reusable UI components.\n    *   `src/admin/pages`: Individual pages for managing users, providers, system configuration, and generating reports.\n    *   `src/admin/services`: API client for interacting with the backend API (with admin privileges).\n\n## Real-time & Events Architecture\n\nThe system utilizes WebSockets and Server-Sent Events (SSE) for real-time updates and event notifications.\n\n*   **WebSockets:** Used for interactive features like live negotiation updates and real-time chat with support agents.\n*   **SSE:** Used for pushing notifications to users about subscription events, such as upcoming renewals, price increases, and new recommendations.\n\nA message queue (e.g., RabbitMQ or Kafka) is used to decouple the services and ensure reliable event delivery.  Events such as `subscription.created`, `subscription.updated`, `transaction.created`, and `recommendation.generated` are published to the message queue and consumed by the appropriate services.\n\n## Authentication & Authorization\n\nAuthentication is handled using JWT (JSON Web Tokens).\n\n*   Users authenticate with their email and password.\n*   Upon successful authentication, the server issues a JWT that is stored in the client's browser.\n*   The JWT is included in the `Authorization` header of subsequent requests.\n*   The API Gateway verifies the JWT and extracts the user ID and roles.\n\nAuthorization is implemented using Role-Based Access Control (RBAC).\n\n*   Each user is assigned one or more roles.\n*   Each role is granted a set of permissions.\n*   The API Gateway uses the user's roles and permissions to determine whether they are authorized to access a particular endpoint.\n\n## Deployment & Scalability\n\nThe system is deployed to a cloud platform (e.g., AWS, Azure, or GCP) using containerization (Docker) and orchestration (Kubernetes).\n\n*   Each microservice is packaged as a Docker container.\n*   Kubernetes is used to manage the deployment, scaling, and networking of the containers.\n*   Load balancers are used to distribute traffic across multiple instances of each service.\n*   The database is configured for high availability and scalability using replication and sharding.\n\nThe system is designed to scale horizontally by adding more instances of each microservice as needed.  Auto-scaling is configured to automatically adjust the number of instances based on traffic and resource utilization.\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\nThe system is tested at multiple levels:\n\n*   **Unit Tests:**  Test individual components and functions in isolation.\n*   **Integration Tests:**  Test the interactions between different components and services.\n*   **End-to-End Tests:**  Test the entire system from the user's perspective.\n*   **API Tests:** Tests the REST API endpoints.\n*   **Performance Tests:**  Measure the performance of the system under different load conditions.\n*   **Security Tests:**  Identify and address security vulnerabilities.\n\nA continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) pipeline is used to automate the testing and deployment process.\n\n## Security & Hardening\n\nSecurity is a top priority.  The following measures are implemented to protect the system:\n\n*   **Authentication and Authorization:**  JWT and RBAC are used to control access to the system.\n*   **Data Encryption:**  Sensitive data is encrypted at rest and in transit.\n*   **Input Validation:**  All user input is validated to prevent injection attacks.\n*   **Regular Security Audits:**  The system is regularly audited for security vulnerabilities.\n*   **Dependency Management:** Dependencies are regularly updated to patch security vulnerabilities.\n*   **Rate Limiting:**  Rate limiting is used to prevent denial-of-service attacks.\n*   **Web Application Firewall (WAF):** A WAF is used to protect the system from common web attacks.\n*   **Penetration Testing:**  Regular penetration testing is conducted to identify and address security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Logging & Observability\n\nComprehensive logging and observability are implemented to monitor the health and performance of the system.\n\n*   **Centralized Logging:**  Logs from all services are aggregated into a central logging system (e.g., ELK stack or Splunk).\n*   **Metrics:**  Key metrics are collected and monitored (e.g., CPU utilization, memory usage, request latency).\n*   **Tracing:**  Distributed tracing is used to track requests across multiple services.\n*   **Alerting:**  Alerts are configured to notify operators of critical issues.\n*   **Health Checks:** Health check endpoints are exposed by each service to monitor their availability.\n*   **Dashboards:**  Dashboards are created to visualize key metrics and logs.\n\n## Features Overview\n\nThe subtrack-ai platform offers a comprehensive suite of features designed to automate and optimize subscription management, ultimately saving users money and simplifying their financial lives. These features can be broadly categorized into subscription tracking, AI-powered recommendations, automated negotiation, and user account management.\n\n**Subscription Tracking & Management:**\n\n1.  **Automatic Subscription Discovery:** Scan user bank statements and email inboxes to identify existing subscriptions.\n2.  **Manual Subscription Entry:** Allows users to manually add and manage subscriptions not automatically detected.\n3.  **Subscription Details View:** Detailed information for each subscription including name, provider, price, renewal date, and payment method.\n4.  **Categorization of Subscriptions:** Automatically categorize subscriptions (e.g., streaming, SaaS, utilities).\n5.  **Subscription Status Tracking:** Monitor subscription status (active, inactive, trial, cancelled).\n6.  **Renewal Reminders:** Notifications sent before upcoming subscription renewals.\n7.  **Spending Overview:** Provides a clear overview of total subscription spending per month/year.\n8.  **Spending Breakdown:** Shows where the subscription money is going through visually pleasing charts.\n9.  **Subscription Cancellation Feature:** Facilitates direct cancellation of subscriptions through the platform (where possible).\n10. **Import Subscription Data:** Users can import data from other subscription management tools.\n11. **Export Subscription Data:** Enables users to export their subscription data in various formats (CSV, JSON).\n\n**AI-Powered Recommendations & Optimization:**\n\n12. **Personalized Subscription Recommendations:** AI-driven suggestions for optimizing existing subscriptions.\n13. **Alternative Plan Suggestions:** Recommends cheaper or more suitable alternative subscription plans.\n14. **Usage Analysis:** Analyzes subscription usage to identify underutilized subscriptions.\n15. **Savings Projections:** Estimates potential savings from implementing recommendations.\n16. **Negotiation Opportunities:** Identifies subscriptions with potential for price negotiation.\n17. **Cost-Benefit Analysis:** Provides a cost-benefit analysis of each subscription.\n18. **Duplicate Subscription Detection:** Identifies duplicate subscriptions for the same service.\n19. **Automated Savings Goals:** Users can set monthly savings goals and track progress.\n20. **Subscription Benchmarking:** Compares subscription costs with similar users.\n\n**Automated Negotiation & Savings:**\n\n21. **Automated Negotiation:** AI-powered negotiation with subscription providers to secure better rates.\n22. **Negotiation History:** Tracks the history of all negotiation attempts.\n23. **Negotiation Status Updates:** Provides real-time updates on the status of negotiation requests.\n24. **Negotiation Outcome Tracking:** Records the outcome of each negotiation (success, failure, partial success).\n25. **Savings Tracking:** Tracks the total savings achieved through negotiation and optimization.\n26. **Integration with Negotiation Services:** Connects with third-party negotiation services.\n\n**User Account Management & Security:**\n\n27. **Secure User Authentication:** Robust authentication system with password encryption.\n28. **Two-Factor Authentication (2FA):** Enhanced security with two-factor authentication.\n29. **User Profile Management:** Allows users to manage their profile information.\n30. **Payment Method Management:** Securely store and manage payment methods.\n31. **Notification Preferences:** Customizable notification settings.\n32. **Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):** Different user roles with specific permissions.\n33. **Audit Logging:** Tracks user activity for security and compliance purposes.\n34. **Data Privacy Compliance:** Adherence to data privacy regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA).\n\n**Reporting & Analytics:**\n\n35. **Subscription Spending Reports:** Provides detailed reports on subscription spending.\n36.  **Savings Reports:** Reports on how much money the system is saving per period.\n37.  **Subscription Usage Reports:** Provides insight into frequency of usage.\n38.  **Customizable Reporting Periods:** Generate reports for specific periods.\n39.  **Exportable Reports:** Reports can be exported in multiple formats.\n\n**Integration & Extensibility:**\n40. **Bank Account Integration:** Links with bank accounts for transaction monitoring.\n41. **Email Integration:** Connects with email inboxes to identify subscriptions.\n42.  **API Access:** Public API for third-party integrations.\n\n## Feature Implementation Roadmap\n\nOkay, let's break down the subtrack-ai project and create a feature implementation roadmap for the 5 most important features.\n\n### 1. Subscription Tracking\n**Description:**  Allows users to manually input and track their existing subscriptions, including details like provider, billing frequency, amount, and renewal date. This forms the foundation for all other features.\n\n**User Story:** As a user, I want to easily add my subscriptions to the system so that I have a central overview of all my recurring expenses.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n- Implement input forms for subscription details (name, provider, amount, frequency, renewal date, category).\n- Validate input data to ensure consistency (e.g., amount is a number, date is in a correct format).\n- Store subscription data securely.\n- Display a list of subscriptions with key details.\n- Allow users to edit and delete subscriptions.\n\n**Database Changes:**\n- Create a `Subscriptions` table with the following fields:\n    - `id` (INT, PRIMARY KEY, AUTO_INCREMENT)\n    - `userId` (INT, FOREIGN KEY referencing `Users.id`)\n    - `name` (VARCHAR)\n    - `provider` (VARCHAR)\n    - `amount` (DECIMAL)\n    - `frequency` (ENUM: 'monthly', 'yearly', 'quarterly', 'weekly')\n    - `renewalDate` (DATE)\n    - `category` (VARCHAR)\n    - `createdAt` (TIMESTAMP)\n    - `updatedAt` (TIMESTAMP)\n\n**Backend Components:**\n- **Model:** `Subscription` (Defines the structure and validation rules for subscription data).\n- **Controller:** `SubscriptionsController` (Handles CRUD operations for subscriptions: create, read, update, delete).\n- **Service:** `SubscriptionsService` (Contains the business logic for interacting with the Subscription model and performing data operations).\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n- `SubscriptionList.tsx`: Displays a list of user's subscriptions.\n- `SubscriptionForm.tsx`: Allows users to add or edit a subscription.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n- `POST /api/subscriptions`: Create a new subscription.\n- `GET /api/subscriptions`: Get all subscriptions for the logged-in user.\n- `GET /api/subscriptions/:id`: Get a specific subscription by ID.\n- `PUT /api/subscriptions/:id`: Update a subscription.\n- `DELETE /api/subscriptions/:id`: Delete a subscription.\n\n### 2. Automated Subscription Detection\n**Description:** Automatically identify subscriptions from connected bank accounts or credit card statements. This reduces manual input and provides a more complete picture.\n\n**User Story:** As a user, I want the system to automatically detect my subscriptions from my bank statements so that I don't have to manually enter them all.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n- Integrate with Plaid or a similar financial data aggregation service.\n- Authenticate users and securely connect to their bank accounts.\n- Analyze transaction data to identify recurring payments.\n- Develop a rule-based or machine learning algorithm to classify transactions as subscriptions.\n- Allow users to confirm or reject automatically detected subscriptions.\n- Handle potential errors and edge cases (e.g., transactions with ambiguous descriptions).\n\n**Database Changes:**\n- Add a `BankAccounts` table with fields:\n    - `id` (INT, PRIMARY KEY, AUTO_INCREMENT)\n    - `userId` (INT, FOREIGN KEY referencing `Users.id`)\n    - `plaidAccessToken` (VARCHAR)  (or relevant access token for the integrated service)\n    - `institutionName` (VARCHAR)\n- Add a `isAutoDetected` (BOOLEAN) field to the `Subscriptions` table.\n\n**Backend Components:**\n- **Service:** `PlaidService` (Handles the integration with Plaid, authenticates users, fetches transaction data).\n- **Service:** `TransactionAnalyzerService` (Analyzes transaction data to identify subscriptions).\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n- `BankAccountConnection.tsx`: Handles the connection to bank accounts via Plaid.\n- `AutoDetectedSubscriptions.tsx`: Displays a list of automatically detected subscriptions and allows users to confirm or reject them.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n- `POST /api/plaid/linkToken`: Generates a link token for Plaid Link.\n- `POST /api/plaid/exchangeToken`: Exchanges a public token for an access token and stores it.\n- `GET /api/transactions`: Retrieves transaction data from Plaid.\n- `POST /api/subscriptions/auto-detect`: Manually trigger the subscription detection service.\n\n### 3.  Subscription Rate Optimization\n**Description:**  Analyzes subscription data to identify potential savings opportunities (e.g., cheaper plans, bundled offers).\n\n**User Story:** As a user, I want the system to identify potential ways to save money on my existing subscriptions so that I can reduce my monthly expenses.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n- Create a database of subscription plans and pricing from various providers.\n- Compare user's current subscriptions to available plans.\n- Identify cheaper alternatives or bundled offers.\n- Potentially integrate with APIs of subscription providers to fetch real-time pricing and plan details.\n\n**Database Changes:**\n- Create a `SubscriptionPlans` table with fields:\n    - `id` (INT, PRIMARY KEY, AUTO_INCREMENT)\n    - `provider` (VARCHAR)\n    - `name` (VARCHAR)\n    - `price` (DECIMAL)\n    - `frequency` (ENUM: 'monthly', 'yearly', 'quarterly', 'weekly')\n    - `features` (TEXT) (JSON array of plan features)\n\n**Backend Components:**\n- **Service:** `OptimizationService` (Analyzes subscription data and recommends optimization strategies).\n- **Repository:** `SubscriptionPlansRepository` (Access the plans details)\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n- `OptimizationSuggestions.tsx`: Displays a list of potential savings opportunities.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n- `GET /api/subscriptions/:id/optimizations`: Get optimization suggestions for a specific subscription.\n\n### 4.  Subscription Cancellation Reminders\n**Description:**  Provides timely reminders before subscription renewals to prevent unwanted charges.\n\n**User Story:** As a user, I want to receive notifications before my subscriptions renew so that I can cancel them if I no longer need them.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n- Schedule notifications based on subscription renewal dates.\n- Allow users to customize notification preferences (e.g., lead time, notification method).\n- Implement a robust notification system (e.g., email, push notifications).\n\n**Database Changes:**\n- Add `notificationLeadTime` (INT) field to the `Subscriptions` table to indicate how many days before renewal to send the notification.\n\n**Backend Components:**\n- **Service:** `NotificationService` (Schedules and sends subscription renewal reminders).\n- **Worker:** Scheduled job (Cron-like) that checks for upcoming renewals and triggers notifications.\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n- `NotificationSettings.tsx`: Allows users to configure their notification preferences.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n- `PUT /api/subscriptions/:id/notification-settings`: Update notification settings for a subscription.\n\n### 5.  Reporting and Analytics\n**Description:**  Provides users with insights into their subscription spending habits, including total monthly expenses, category breakdowns, and trends over time.\n\n**User Story:** As a user, I want to see a clear overview of my subscription spending so that I can understand where my money is going and make informed decisions.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n- Aggregate subscription data.\n- Generate visualizations (charts, graphs) to represent spending trends.\n- Provide filtering and sorting options.\n\n**Database Changes:**\n- No new database changes needed, as this feature utilizes existing subscription data.\n\n**Backend Components:**\n- **Service:** `AnalyticsService` (Calculates and formats subscription data for reporting).\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n- `Dashboard.tsx`: Displays a summary of subscription spending.\n- `SpendingTrends.tsx`: Visualizes spending trends over time.\n- `CategoryBreakdown.tsx`: Displays a breakdown of spending by subscription category.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n- `GET /api/analytics/summary`: Get a summary of subscription spending.\n- `GET /api/analytics/trends`: Get spending trends over time.\n- `GET /api/analytics/categories`: Get a breakdown of spending by category.\n\nThese 5 features provide a solid foundation for the subtrack-ai project.  The roadmap can be further refined by prioritizing features within each category and breaking down each feature into smaller, more manageable tasks. Remember to prioritize security and user experience throughout the development process.\n",
