          "raw_content": "# micronutrient-optimizer Architecture\n\n## Overview\nAI-powered platform for personalized micronutrient tracking & optimization. Improve health with data-driven food & supplement recommendations.\n\n### Problem Statement\nIndividuals struggle to consistently track and manage their micronutrient intake, leading to potential deficiencies and suboptimal health.\n\n### Target Audience\nHealth-conscious individuals, athletes, people with dietary restrictions, and anyone seeking to improve their health through personalized nutrition guidance.\n\n### Core Entities\n\n- **User**: Represents an individual using the application with authentication credentials, profile information, and dietary preferences.\n- **Food**: Represents a food item with nutritional information including macronutrients and micronutrients.\n- **FoodIntakeLog**: Records of food consumed by users with timestamps, quantities, and linked nutritional data.\n- **Micronutrient**: Individual vitamins and minerals with RDI (Recommended Daily Intake) values and health impact information.\n- **MicronutrientAnalysis**: Calculated analysis of user's micronutrient intake compared to recommended values.\n- **Supplement**: Dietary supplements with their micronutrient composition and dosage information.\n- **Recipe**: Collection of food items that can be logged as a single meal with combined nutritional values.\n- **HealthProfile**: User's health data including age, gender, weight, height, activity level, and health conditions that affect nutritional needs.\n\n## Design System\n\n### Typography\n- **Font**: Inter\n- **Font Stack**: `'Inter', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif`\n- **Google Fonts**: https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Inter\n- **Rationale**: Inter is a modern, highly readable font specifically designed and optimized for use on computer screens. Its clean lines and generous spacing contribute to a comfortable reading experience, especially important for extended use cases like micronutrient tracking. [https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Inter]\n\n### Color Palette\nThis professional color palette prioritizes accessibility by ensuring strong contrast ratios between text and background colors, adhering to WCAG AA guidelines. The logical structure groups colors by function, making it easier to maintain consistency throughout the application. Colors have been chosen to evoke feelings of trust and reliability.\n\n- **Primary Blue**: `#0066CC` - Primary actions and branding. Used for buttons, links, and the main application color theme.\n- **Secondary Gray**: `#6B7280` - Secondary text and elements. Used for less important text, icons, and dividers.\n- **Accent Orange**: `#F59E0B` - Call-to-action and highlights. Used sparingly for important actions to draw attention.\n- **Dark Gray**: `#111827` - Primary text. Used for most of the text content in the application.\n- **Light Gray**: `#F9FAFB` - Background. Used for the main application background and larger content areas.\n- **Border Gray**: `#E5E7EB` - Borders and dividers. Used for separating content and creating visual structure.\n- **Green**: `#10B981` - Success states. Used for indicating successful actions and positive feedback.\n- **Yellow**: `#F59E0B` - Warning messages. Used for indicating potential problems or issues that need attention.\n- **Red**: `#EF4444` - Error states. Used for indicating errors and critical issues.\n\n## System Architecture\n\n### 1. Core Components & Rationale\nThe architecture consists of:\n1.  **Laravel Backend:** Handles API requests, database interactions (MySQL), user authentication (Sanctum), background processing (Queues), and real-time communication (Laravel Echo Server/Redis). Laravel provides a robust framework for building scalable and maintainable APIs.\n2.  **React Frontend (TypeScript/Inertia.js):** Provides the user interface, handles user interactions, and displays data fetched from the backend. TypeScript enhances code quality and maintainability, while Inertia.js simplifies routing and data fetching.\n3.  **MySQL Database:** Stores all application data, including user profiles, food products, micronutrient information, and intake logs. MySQL is chosen for its wide adoption, performance, and ease of management.\n4.  **Redis Caching Layer:** Used for caching frequently accessed data and managing real-time communication through Laravel Echo Server. Redis improves performance and reduces the load on the MySQL database.\n5.  **Laravel Echo Server:** WebSocket server for broadcasting real-time events from the backend to connected clients. Enables real-time updates for features like food intake logging and micronutrient analysis. [https://laravel.com/docs/10.x/broadcasting]\n\nEach component is designed to be loosely coupled, allowing for independent scaling and updates. The architecture follows a layered approach, separating concerns and improving maintainability.\n\n### 2. Database Schema Design\nThe MySQL database will have the following key tables:\n\n1.  **users:** Stores user information (id, name, email, password, created_at, updated_at, profile_id, role_id). `role_id` is a foreign key to the `roles` table.\n2.  **profiles:** Stores user profile details (id, user_id (FK), age, gender, weight, height, activity_level, dietary_restrictions, created_at, updated_at).\n3.  **products:** Stores food product information (id, name, brand, serving_size, micronutrient_data (JSON), created_at, updated_at).\n4.  **food_intake_logs:** Stores user's food intake records (id, user_id (FK), product_id (FK), serving_quantity, intake_date, created_at, updated_at).\n5.  **supplements:** Stores vetted supplement information (id, name, brand, dosage_recommendations, micronutrient_composition (JSON), created_at, updated_at).\n6.  **supplement_recommendations:** Stores personalized supplement recommendations (id, user_id (FK), supplement_id (FK), recommended_dosage, created_at, updated_at).\n7.  **settings:** Stores user-specific settings (id, user_id (FK), theme, notification_preferences, created_at, updated_at).\n8.  **roles:** Stores role information (id, name, created_at, updated_at). Example roles: `super_admin`, `admin`, `moderator`, `support`, `user`.\n9.  **permissions:** Stores permission information (id, name, created_at, updated_at). Example permissions: `create_user`, `edit_user`, `delete_user`, `view_analytics`, `moderate_content`.\n10. **role_permissions:** Stores the relationship between roles and permissions (role_id (FK), permission_id (FK)).\n11. **admin_action_logs:** Stores audit logs of admin actions (id, user_id (FK), action, model, model_id, created_at).\n12. **user_activity_logs:** Stores logs of user activities (id, user_id (FK), action, details (JSON), created_at).\n13. **system_configurations:** Stores system-wide configurations (id, key, value, created_at, updated_at).\n\nRelationships:\n*   One-to-one relationship between users and profiles.\n*   One-to-many relationship between users and food_intake_logs.\n*   One-to-many relationship between food_intake_logs and products.\n*   One-to-many relationship between users and supplement_recommendations.\n*   One-to-many relationship between supplement_recommendations and supplements.\n*   One-to-many relationship between users and settings.\n*   One-to-many relationship between users and admin_action_logs (for admin users).\n*   One-to-many relationship between users and user_activity_logs.\n*   One-to-many relationship between roles and users.\n*   Many-to-many relationship between roles and permissions (through role_permissions).\n\nLaravel migrations will be used to manage database schema changes and ensure version control. All foreign keys will have appropriate indexes to optimize query performance. [https://laravel.com/docs/10.x/migrations]\n\n### 3. API Design & Key Endpoints\n\nThe application will expose a RESTful API using Laravel Sanctum for authentication. API endpoints will follow REST conventions and use appropriate HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) and status codes.  API rate limiting will be implemented using Laravel's built-in rate limiting middleware, configured differently for admin and user roles.\n\n**Admin API Endpoints:** (Requires `auth:sanctum` and `role:admin,super_admin,moderator,support` middleware)\n\n*   **User Management:**\n    *   `GET /api/admin/users`: Get all users (with pagination, filtering, and sorting). Requires `view_user` permission.\n    *   `GET /api/admin/users/{id}`: Get a specific user. Requires `view_user` permission.\n    *   `POST /api/admin/users`: Create a new user. Requires `create_user` permission.\n    *   `PUT /api/admin/users/{id}`: Update a user. Requires `edit_user` permission.\n    *   `DELETE /api/admin/users/{id}`: Delete a user. Requires `delete_user` permission.\n    *   `POST /api/admin/users/{id}/assign-role`: Assign a role to a user. Requires `assign_role` permission.\n    *   `POST /api/admin/users/{id}/permissions`: Assign specific permissions to a user (overriding role permissions). Requires `edit_permission` permission.\n    *   `GET /api/admin/users/{id}/roles`: Get the roles of a specific user.\n    *   `GET /api/admin/users/{id}/permissions`: Get the permissions of a specific user.\n*   **System Configuration:**\n    *   `GET /api/admin/configs`: Get all system configurations. Requires `view_config` permission.\n    *   `GET /api/admin/configs/{key}`: Get a specific system configuration. Requires `view_config` permission.\n    *   `PUT /api/admin/configs/{key}`: Update a system configuration. Requires `edit_config` permission.\n*   **Analytics and Reporting:**\n    *   `GET /api/admin/analytics/user-growth`: Get user growth analytics. Requires `view_analytics` permission.\n    *   `GET /api/admin/analytics/usage-statistics`: Get application usage statistics. Requires `view_analytics` permission.\n    *   `GET /api/admin/reports/food-intake`: Generate a report on food intake trends. Requires `view_reports` permission.\n    *   `GET /api/admin/reports/nutrient-deficiencies`: Generate a report on common nutrient deficiencies. Requires `view_reports` permission.\n*   **Content Moderation:**\n    *   `GET /api/admin/moderation/food-products`: Get a list of food products pending moderation. Requires `moderate_content` permission.\n    *   `PUT /api/admin/moderation/food-products/{id}/approve`: Approve a food product. Requires `moderate_content` permission.\n    *   `PUT /api/admin/moderation/food-products/{id}/reject`: Reject a food product. Requires `moderate_content` permission.\n    *   `GET /api/admin/moderation/user-reports`: Get a list of user reports. Requires `moderate_content` permission.\n    *   `POST /api/admin/moderation/user-reports/{id}/resolve`: Resolve a user report. Requires `moderate_content` permission.\n*   **Roles and Permissions:**\n    *   `GET /api/admin/roles`: List all roles. Requires `view_role` permission.\n    *   `GET /api/admin/roles/{id}`: Get details of a specific role. Requires `view_role` permission.\n    *   `POST /api/admin/roles`: Create a new role. Requires `create_role` permission.\n    *   `PUT /api/admin/roles/{id}`: Update a role. Requires `edit_role` permission.\n    *   `DELETE /api/admin/roles/{id}`: Delete a role. Requires `delete_role` permission.\n    *   `POST /api/admin/roles/{id}/permissions`: Assign permissions to a role. Requires `assign_permission` permission.\n    *   `GET /api/admin/permissions`: List all permissions. Requires `view_permission` permission.\n*   **Audit Logs:**\n    *   `GET /api/admin/audit-logs`: Get admin action audit logs (with filtering and pagination). Requires `view_audit_logs` permission.\n\n**User API Endpoints:** (Requires `auth:sanctum` middleware)\n\n*   **Authentication:**\n    *   `POST /api/register`: User registration.\n    *   `POST /api/login`: User login.\n    *   `POST /api/logout`: User logout.\n    *   `GET /api/user`: Get authenticated user information.\n    *   `POST /api/reset-password`: Initiate password reset (sends email).\n    *   `POST /api/update-password`: Update password with token.\n*   **Profile:**\n    *   `GET /api/profile`: Get user profile.\n    *   `PUT /api/profile`: Update user profile.\n*   **Products:**\n    *   `GET /api/products`: Get all products (with pagination).\n    *   `GET /api/products/{id}`: Get a specific product.\n    *   `POST /api/products/search`: Search for products by name.\n    *   `POST /api/products/report`: Report an issue with a food product (sends to moderation).\n*   **Food Intake Logs:**\n    *   `GET /api/food-intake-logs`: Get user's food intake logs (with date filtering).\n    *   `POST /api/food-intake-logs`: Create a new food intake log.\n    *   `PUT /api/food-intake-logs/{id}`: Update a food intake log.\n    *   `DELETE /api/food-intake-logs/{id}`: Delete a food intake log.\n*   **Micronutrient Analysis:**\n    *   `GET /api/micronutrient-analysis`: Analyze micronutrient intake based on food intake logs.\n*   **Supplement Recommendations:**\n    *   `GET /api/supplement-recommendations`: Get personalized supplement recommendations.\n*   **Settings:**\n    *   `GET /api/settings`: Get user settings.\n    *   `PUT /api/settings`: Update user settings.\n*   **User Activity:**\n     *   `GET /api/activity-logs`: Retrieve user's recent activity logs (pagination).\n*   **Subscription (Future Enhancement):**\n    *   `POST /api/subscribe`: Subscribe to a premium plan.\n    *   `POST /api/unsubscribe`: Unsubscribe from the current plan.\n    *   `GET  /api/subscription`: Get subscription details\nAll API endpoints will be protected by authentication middleware, ensuring that only authenticated users can access their own data. Input validation will be implemented using Laravel's validation rules to prevent invalid data from being stored in the database. [https://laravel.com/docs/10.x/sanctum]\n\n**Admin Authentication Flow:**\n\n1.  Admin submits login form with email and password.\n2.  Backend authenticates the admin user.\n3.  Backend checks if 2FA is enabled. If not, generates an API token using Sanctum.\n4.  If 2FA is enabled, generates a temporary token and sends a 2FA code to the admin's registered email/phone.\n5.  Admin submits the 2FA code.\n6.  Backend validates the 2FA code.\n7.  If the 2FA code is valid, generates an API token using Sanctum.\n8.  The frontend stores the API token in a secure cookie.\n\n**User Authentication Flow:**\n\n1. User submits login form with email and password.\n2. Backend authenticates the user and generates an API token using Sanctum.\n3. The frontend stores the API token in local storage.\n\n### 4. Frontend Structure (TypeScript)\nThe React frontend will be structured using feature-based folders. Each feature (e.g., Profile, FoodIntake, MicronutrientAnalysis) will have its own folder containing components, hooks, and styles.  The frontend structure is now separated into `admin` and `user` portals.\n\n*   **`src/`:**\n    *   **`components/`:** Reusable UI components (e.g., Button, Input, Card).\n    *   **`layouts/`**: Common layout components (e.g., DefaultLayout)\n    *   **`admin/`:** Admin portal components and logic.\n        *   **`components/admin/`:**\n            *   **`Dashboard/`:**\n                *   `AdminDashboard.tsx`: Admin dashboard component.\n                *   `AnalyticsCard.tsx`: Component for displaying analytics data.\n            *   **`UserManagement/`:**\n                *   `UserList.tsx`: Component for listing users.\n                *   `UserEditForm.tsx`: Component for editing user information.\n                *   `UserCreateForm.tsx`: Component for creating new users.\n                *   `RoleAssignment.tsx`: Component for assigning roles to users.\n            *   **`ContentModeration/`:**\n                *   `FoodProductList.tsx`: Component for listing food products pending moderation.\n                *   `FoodProductDetails.tsx`: Component for displaying food product details.\n                *   `UserReportsList.tsx`: Component for listing user reports.\n            *   **`SystemConfiguration/`:**\n                *   `ConfigurationList.tsx`: Component for listing system configurations.\n                *   `ConfigurationEditForm.tsx`: Component for editing system configurations.\n            *   **`AuditLogs/`**:\n                 *   `AuditLogList.tsx`: Component listing admin action audit logs.\n            *   **`layouts/`:**\n                *   `AdminLayout.tsx`: Admin-specific layout with navigation and header.\n        *   **`hooks/`:** Admin-specific custom hooks (e.g., `useAdminUsers`, `useAdminConfigs`).\n    *   **`user/`:** User portal components and logic.\n        *   **`components/user/`:**\n            *   **`Profile/`:**\n                *   `Profile.tsx`: Profile component.\n                *   `EditProfileForm.tsx`: Edit profile form component.\n            *   **`FoodIntake/`:**\n                *   `FoodIntakeLog.tsx`: Food intake log component.\n                *   `AddFoodIntakeForm.tsx`: Add food intake form component.\n            *   **`MicronutrientAnalysis/`:**\n                *   `MicronutrientAnalysis.tsx`: Micronutrient analysis component.\n            *   **`SupplementRecommendations/`:**\n                *   `SupplementRecommendations.tsx`: Supplement recommendations component.\n            *   **`layouts/`:**\n                *   `UserLayout.tsx`: User-specific layout.\n        *   **`hooks/`:** User-specific custom hooks (e.g., `useProfile`, `useFoodIntake`).\n    *   **`hooks/`:** Reusable custom hooks.\n    *   **`services/`:** API client and data fetching logic.\n    *   **`types/`:** TypeScript type definitions.\n    *   **`utils/`:** Utility functions.\n    *   **`App.tsx`:** Main application component, handles routing between admin and user portals.\n    *   **`index.tsx`:** Entry point for the React application.\n\nWe will utilize custom hooks for data fetching, state management, and side effects. The team should utilize a UI library like `shadcn/ui` for pre-built accessible components.  [https://ui.shadcn.com/]\n\nTypeScript will be used throughout the frontend to ensure type safety and improve code quality. Strict mode will be enabled in the `tsconfig.json` file to catch potential errors early on.\n\n### 5. Real-time & Events Architecture\nReal-time functionality will be implemented using Redis pub/sub and Laravel Echo Server. When a user adds a food intake log, the backend will dispatch an event (`FoodIntakeCreated`). Laravel Echo Server will listen for this event and broadcast it to all connected clients (browsers).\n\nThe frontend will subscribe to the `FoodIntakeCreated` event using Laravel Echo and React's `useState` hook. When the event is received, the frontend will update the food intake log in real-time without requiring a page refresh. Admin notifications will also be implemented using this architecture. For example, when a user reports a food product, the backend will dispatch an event (`FoodProductReported`), and the admin portal will receive a real-time notification.\n\nComponents:\n*   **Redis:** Used as a message broker for pub/sub.\n*   **Laravel Echo Server:** WebSocket server for broadcasting events.\n*   **Laravel Events:** Used to define and dispatch events from the backend.\n*   **Laravel Broadcasting:** Used to configure the broadcasting driver (Redis) and channels.\n*   **React with Laravel Echo:** Used to subscribe to events and update the UI in real-time.\n\nBenefits:\n*   Real-time updates for food intake logs and micronutrient analysis.\n*   Real-time admin notifications for moderation tasks.\n*   Improved user experience.\n*   Reduced server load compared to polling. [https://laravel.com/docs/10.x/broadcasting]\n\n### 6. Authentication & Authorization Flow\nLaravel Sanctum will be used for SPA authentication. The authentication flow will be as follows:\n\n1.  **Registration:** User submits registration form with name, email, and password. The backend validates the input and creates a new user record in the database.\n2.  **Login:** User submits login form with email and password. The backend authenticates the user and generates an API token using Sanctum.\n3.  **API Access:** The frontend stores the API token in local storage or a secure cookie. The token is included in the `Authorization` header of all API requests.\n4.  **Authentication Middleware:** The backend uses the `auth:sanctum` middleware to authenticate API requests. The middleware verifies the API token and grants access to protected resources.\n5.  **Logout:** User clicks the logout button. The frontend removes the API token from local storage and revokes the token on the backend.\n\n**Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):**\n\n*   Roles: `super_admin`, `admin`, `moderator`, `support`, `user`.\n*   Permissions: Defined in the `permissions` table (e.g., `create_user`, `edit_user`, `delete_user`, `view_analytics`, `moderate_content`).\n*   Middleware: A custom middleware `CheckRole` will be used to protect admin routes and ensure that the user has the required role and permissions.  Example: `Route::middleware(['auth:sanctum', 'role:admin,super_admin', 'permission:edit_user'])`\n*   Spatie Permissions package will be used to simplify RBAC implementation. [https://spatie.be/docs/laravel-permission/v5/introduction]\n\nAuthorization:\n*   Laravel Policies will be used to define authorization rules for accessing and manipulating resources. For example, a user can only update their own profile or delete their own food intake logs.\n*   Admin actions will be logged in the `admin_action_logs` table for auditing purposes.\n\n**Redis Configuration:**\n\n*   **Admin Session Management:** Redis will be used to store admin sessions for improved performance and scalability.\n*   **User Session Management:** Redis will be used to store user sessions.\n*   **Real-time Admin Notifications:** Redis pub/sub will be used to broadcast real-time notifications to the admin portal.\n*   **Cache Strategies:**\n    *   Admin data (e.g., user list, system configurations) will be cached aggressively in Redis to reduce database load.  Cache invalidation will be implemented using Laravel's cache tags.\n    *   User data (e.g., profile, food intake logs) will be cached with shorter TTLs to ensure data freshness.\n\n### 7. Deployment & Scalability Plan\nThe application will be deployed using Docker containers to ensure consistency across different environments. The deployment pipeline will consist of the following steps:\n\n1.  **Build Docker Images:** Create Docker images for the Laravel backend, React frontend, MySQL database, and Redis server.\n2.  **Push Images to Container Registry:** Push the Docker images to a container registry like Docker Hub or AWS ECR.\n3.  **Deploy to Cloud Platform:** Deploy the Docker containers to a cloud platform like AWS ECS, Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), or Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS).\n4.  **Configure Load Balancer:** Configure a load balancer to distribute traffic across multiple instances of the Laravel backend and React frontend.\n5.  **Configure Database and Redis:** Configure the MySQL database and Redis server to be accessible from the Laravel backend.\n\nScalability:\n*   **Horizontal Scaling:** The Laravel backend and React frontend can be scaled horizontally by adding more instances behind the load balancer.\n*   **Database Scaling:** The MySQL database can be scaled by using read replicas to distribute read traffic and improve performance.\n*   **Redis Clustering:** Redis can be clustered to provide high availability and scalability.\n*   **Queue Workers:** Increase the number of queue workers to handle background tasks more efficiently.\n\nMonitoring:\n*   Implement monitoring tools to track key metrics such as CPU usage, memory usage, and response time. Set up alerts to notify the team of any issues.  [https://laravel.com/docs/10.x/queues]\n\n### 8. Testing Strategy\nA comprehensive testing strategy will be implemented to ensure the quality and reliability of the application.\n\n*   **Backend (Laravel):**\n    *   **Pest:** Used for writing unit tests, feature tests, and integration tests.\n    *   **Code Coverage:** Aim for a minimum of 80% code coverage.\n    *   **Test-Driven Development (TDD):** Encourage developers to write tests before writing code.\n    *   **Database Migrations:** Test database migrations to ensure they are working correctly.\n    *   **API Tests:** Write tests to verify the functionality of the API endpoints.\n    *   **RBAC Tests:** Write tests to verify that the RBAC implementation is working correctly and that users only have access to the resources they are authorized to access.\n*   **Frontend (React/TypeScript):**\n    *   **Vitest:** Used for writing unit tests and component tests.\n    *   **React Testing Library (RTL):** Used for testing React components in a user-centric way.\n    *   **End-to-End (E2E) Tests:** Cypress or Playwright can be used for E2E tests to verify the entire application flow.\n    *   **Component Isolation:** Use Storybook to develop and test React components in isolation.\n    *   **Admin Portal Tests:** Write specific tests for the admin portal to ensure that admin functionality is working correctly.\n\nAutomated testing will be integrated into the CI/CD pipeline to ensure that all tests pass before deploying code to production. [https://pestphp.com/], [https://vitest.dev/], [https://testing-library.com/]\n\n### 9. Security & Hardening Plan\nA security-first approach will be adopted to protect the application from common web vulnerabilities. The following security measures will be implemented:\n\n*   **OWASP Top 10 Compliance:** Address all OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities.\n*   **Input Validation:** Validate all user input on both the client-side and server-side to prevent SQL injection, XSS, and other attacks.\n*   **SQL Injection Prevention:** Use parameterized queries or an ORM (Eloquent) to prevent SQL injection attacks.\n*   **XSS Protection:** Sanitize all user-generated content before displaying it on the page to prevent XSS attacks.\n*   **CSRF Protection:** Use CSRF tokens to protect against cross-site request forgery attacks.  Laravel automatically handles CSRF protection. [https://owasp.org/Top10/]\n*   **Authentication and Authorization:** Use strong authentication and authorization mechanisms to protect sensitive data.\n*   **Password Hashing:** Use bcrypt or Argon2 to hash passwords.\n*   **Rate Limiting:** Implement rate limiting to prevent brute-force attacks. Apply stricter rate limiting to admin API endpoints.  Use Laravel's built-in rate limiting middleware and customize the configuration for different roles.\n*   **Regular Security Audits:** Conduct regular security audits to identify and fix vulnerabilities.\n*   **Dependency Updates:** Keep all dependencies up to date to patch security vulnerabilities.\n*   **Secure Configuration:** Securely configure the web server and database to prevent unauthorized access.\n*   **HTTPS:** Enforce HTTPS to encrypt all traffic between the client and server.\n*   **Content Security Policy (CSP):** Implement a CSP to prevent XSS attacks and other malicious content from being loaded. [https://content-security-policy.com/]\n*   **Two-Factor Authentication (2FA):** Implement 2FA for admin users to provide an extra layer of security.  Use a package like `laravel-fortify` or implement custom 2FA logic.\n*   **Admin Action Logging:** Log all admin actions in the `admin_action_logs` table to track changes and identify potential security breaches.\n*   **User Activity Tracking:** Track user activities in the `user_activity_logs` table to monitor user behavior and detect suspicious activity.\n\n### 10. Logging & Observability\nComprehensive logging and observability are crucial for monitoring the health and performance of the application. The following measures will be implemented:\n\n*   **Structured Logging:** Use structured logging to log events in a consistent format (e.g., JSON).\n*   **Laravel's Log Channels:** Configure Laravel's log channels to send logs to different destinations (e.g., file, database, stderr, cloudwatch).\n*   **Application Metrics:** Collect application metrics such as request latency, error rate, and CPU usage.\n*   **Performance Monitoring:** Use tools like New Relic or Datadog to monitor the performance of the application and identify bottlenecks.\n*   **Error Tracking:** Use tools like Sentry to track errors and exceptions in real-time.\n*   **Log Rotation:** Configure log rotation to prevent log files from growing too large.\n*   **Centralized Logging:** Send logs to a centralized logging system like Elasticsearch or Splunk for analysis and visualization.\n*   **Audit Logging:** Log all admin actions, including user modifications, system configuration changes, and content moderation activities. Capture details such as the user performing the action, the timestamp, and the specific changes made.\n\nSpecific Configurations:\n*   Utilize the `stderr` channel to output log messages directly to the standard error stream. This is best practice for containerized environments because the container runtime handles redirecting `stderr` to a persistent log.\n*   Implement contextual logging by adding user information, request IDs, and other relevant data to each log message.\n*   Set up dashboards to visualize key metrics and identify trends. [https://laravel.com/docs/10.x/logging]\n\n## Features Overview\n\nHere's a comprehensive list of planned features for the micronutrient-optimizer project:\n\n**Core Features (User):**\n\n1.  **Food Intake Logging (Manual Entry):** Allows users to manually record their food consumption.\n2.  **Food Intake Logging (API Integration):** Integrates with external food databases (e.g., Nutritionix, Edamam) for easier food entry.\n3.  **Micronutrient Analysis:** Analyzes user's dietary intake and identifies potential deficiencies or surpluses in micronutrients.\n4.  **Personalized Food Recommendations:** Provides tailored food suggestions based on user's dietary needs and preferences.\n5.  **Supplement Suggestion Module:** Recommends suitable supplements to address specific micronutrient deficiencies.\n6.  **User Profile Management:** Enables users to manage their personal health information, dietary restrictions, and preferences.\n7.  **Custom Recipe Import:** Ability to import recipes from URLs and parse out ingredients for logging.\n8.  **Barcode Scanner Integration:** Scan product barcodes for quick food intake logging.\n9.  **Progress Tracking & Visualization:** Visual representation of micronutrient intake over time, allowing users to track their progress.\n10. **Meal Planning Tools:** Provides meal planning tools to help users create balanced meals that meet their micronutrient needs.\n11. **Dietary Restriction Handling:**  Allows users to specify dietary restrictions (e.g., vegetarian, vegan, gluten-free) for tailored recommendations.\n12. **Recipe Database:**  A curated database of healthy recipes that are high in specific micronutrients.\n13. **Micronutrient Goals Configuration**: Allows users to set custom micronutrient intake goals.\n14. **Integration with Wearable Devices:** Syncs data from wearable devices to automatically track activity levels and energy expenditure.\n15. **Community Forum:** Provides a platform for users to connect, share recipes, and discuss micronutrient-related topics.\n\n**Admin Features:**\n\n1.  **User Management (CRUD):** Allows administrators to create, read, update, and delete user accounts.\n2.  **Role and Permission Management:** Enables administrators to manage user roles and permissions.\n3.  **System Configuration:** Allows administrators to configure system-wide settings.\n4.  **Analytics and Reporting:** Provides administrators with insights into user behavior, usage statistics, and micronutrient deficiency trends.\n5.  **Content Moderation:** Allows administrators to moderate user-generated content, such as food product reviews and forum posts.\n6.  **Supplement Data Management:**  Allows administrators to manage supplement information, dosage recommendations, and potential side effects.\n7.  **Food Database Management:** Enables administrators to manage the food database, ensuring accurate and up-to-date nutrient information.\n8.  **User Report Management:** Allows administrators to handle user reports (e.g. flagging offensive content).\n9.  **Subscription Management:** Manage user subscriptions and payment plans.\n\n**Advanced Features:**\n\n1.  **AI-Powered Personalized Recommendations:** Employs machine learning algorithms to provide more accurate and personalized food and supplement recommendations.\n2.  **Genetic Data Integration:** Integrates with genetic testing services to provide personalized micronutrient recommendations based on individual genetic predispositions.\n3.  **Predictive Analysis:** Uses predictive models to forecast potential micronutrient deficiencies based on user's dietary habits and lifestyle factors.\n4.  **Gamification:** Incorporates gamification elements, such as badges, points, and leaderboards, to encourage user engagement and adherence to dietary recommendations.\n5.  **Telehealth Integration:** Integrates with telehealth platforms to allow users to connect with registered dietitians and nutritionists for personalized counseling.\n6.  **Advanced Reporting:** Allows users to generate custom reports on their micronutrient intake and progress.\n7.  **Customizable Dashboards:** Personalized dashboards that allow users to track their micronutrient intake and progress toward their goals.\n8.  **Smart Reminders:** Intelligent reminders that encourage users to log their food intake and take their supplements.\n9.  **Multi-Language Support:**  Support for multiple languages to cater to a global user base.\n10. **Offline Mode:**  Allows users to access basic features and log data even when they are offline.\n\n**Integration Features:**\n\n1.  **External Food Tracking APIs (Nutritionix, Edamam):** Integrates with external food tracking APIs to streamline food logging.\n2.  **Wearable Device Integration (Fitbit, Apple Health):** Integrates with wearable devices to automatically track activity levels and energy expenditure.\n3.  **Genetic Testing Services Integration (23andMe):** Integrates with genetic testing services to provide personalized micronutrient recommendations.\n4.  **Telehealth Platforms:** Integrate with telehealth platforms for remote consultations with dieticians.\n5.  **Payment Gateways (Stripe, PayPal):** Integrate with payment gateways for subscription payments.\n\n## Feature Implementation Roadmap\n\n### 1. Food intake logging (manual entry and integration with food tracking APIs).\nImplement a feature for users to manually enter their food intake and integrate with external food tracking APIs (e.g., Nutritionix, Edamam) to automatically log food intake based on search queries.\n\n**Description:** This feature enables users to record their daily food consumption by either manually entering details or leveraging integrations with established food databases. It streamlines the tracking process and promotes user engagement.\n\n**User Story:** As a user, I want to easily log my food intake, either manually or by searching a database, so I can accurately track my micronutrient consumption.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n*   Develop a user interface for manual food entry with fields for food name, quantity, and serving size.\n*   Integrate with the Nutritionix and Edamam APIs using Guzzle HTTP client in Laravel.\n*   Create API endpoints to handle food intake logging requests and data retrieval.\n*   Implement search functionality to find food items within the integrated food databases.\n*   Store food intake logs in the `food_intake_logs` table with relevant details.\n*   Implement API rate limiting to prevent abuse of external API resources.\n\n**Database Changes:**\n- Create `food_intake_logs` table with columns: `id`, `user_id` (FK to `users`), `product_id` (FK to `products`), `serving_quantity`, `intake_date`, `created_at`, `updated_at`.\n\n**Backend Components:**\n- Create `FoodIntakeLog` model.\n- Create `FoodIntakeLogController` to handle API requests for creating, reading, updating, and deleting food intake logs.\n- Create `FoodIntakeLogService` to encapsulate business logic related to food intake logs.\n- Implement API integrations with Nutritionix and Edamam using Guzzle HTTP client.\n- Create a `FoodIntakeCreated` event to be dispatched when a new food intake log is created.\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n**Frontend Components:**\n- Create `FoodIntakeLog.tsx`: Component to display the user's food intake log. Should include features for viewing entries, editing existing entries, and deleting entries.\n- Create `FoodEntryForm.tsx`: Component to handle adding new food entries. Includes fields for food name, serving size, date, and time. Will leverage autocomplete for food names and validation for serving sizes.\n- Create `FoodSearch.tsx`: A searchable dropdown component to allow users to quickly find and select food items. Leverages the food database API for search results.\n- Create `FoodIntakeSummary.tsx`: Component to display a summary of the user's daily caloric and macronutrient intake based on their logged food.\n- Create `styles/FoodIntake.css`: Stylesheet for all food intake components.\n\n---\n\n**Feature 2: Micronutrient analysis based on food intake**\n\n*   **Description:** Analyze the user's food intake to provide insights into their micronutrient consumption (vitamins and minerals). Highlights deficiencies and excesses.\n\n*   **User Story:** As a user, I want to see a breakdown of my micronutrient intake based on my food logs, so I can understand if I'm meeting my daily requirements and identify potential deficiencies.\n\n*   **Technical Requirements:**\n    *   Ability to calculate micronutrient content based on food entries and serving sizes.\n    *   Integration with a comprehensive micronutrient database linked to food items.\n    *   Display of micronutrient intake as a percentage of recommended daily intake (RDI).\n    *   Visual cues (e.g., color-coding) to highlight deficiencies and excesses.\n\n*   **Database Changes:**\n    *   Extend the `foods` table to include micronutrient information (vitamins, minerals, etc.). Consider using a JSON field or creating separate tables for detailed micronutrient data to handle variations and future additions.\n    * Example JSON for micronutrient data in the `foods` table:\n    ```json\n    {\n      \"Vitamin A\": {\"unit\": \"mcg\", \"value\": 500},\n      \"Vitamin C\": {\"unit\": \"mg\", \"value\": 70},\n      \"Iron\": {\"unit\": \"mg\", \"value\": 2},\n      \"Calcium\": {\"unit\": \"mg\", \"value\": 200}\n      //... more micronutrients\n    }\n    ```\n    *   Consider a new table to store Recommended Daily Intake (RDI) values for various micronutrients, potentially linked to user profile data (age, gender, etc.) for personalized recommendations.\n\n*   **Backend Components:**\n    *   Create a `MicronutrientAnalyzer` service that takes a list of `FoodIntake` objects as input.\n    *   The `MicronutrientAnalyzer` service calculates the total micronutrient intake for each micronutrient based on food composition and serving sizes.\n    *   The service compares the calculated intake with RDI values (fetched from the database or a configuration file) and flags deficiencies and excesses.\n    *   Create a new endpoint in the `FoodIntakeController` to trigger the micronutrient analysis.\n\n*   **Frontend Components:**\n    *   Create `MicronutrientReport.tsx`: Displays the user's micronutrient intake as a percentage of RDI.\n    *   Create `MicronutrientChart.tsx`: A visual representation (e.g., bar chart) of the user's micronutrient intake, highlighting deficiencies and excesses.\n    *   Create `MicronutrientDeficiencyAlert.tsx`: Component to display alerts or warnings about significant micronutrient deficiencies.  This could be integrated into the `MicronutrientReport`.\n    *   Add a button/link on the `FoodIntakeSummary.tsx` to navigate to the `MicronutrientReport.tsx`.\n\n*   **API Endpoints:**\n    *   `GET /api/foodintake/{user_id}/micronutrients`: Returns a JSON object containing the user's micronutrient analysis report. The report includes the intake of each micronutrient, the RDI, and a status (e.g., \"deficient,\" \"adequate,\" \"excessive\").\n\n---\n\n**Feature 3: Personalized food recommendations**\n\n*   **Description:** Provide personalized food recommendations based on the user's dietary needs, preferences, and identified micronutrient deficiencies.\n\n*   **User Story:** As a user, I want to receive food recommendations tailored to my dietary needs and preferences, so I can easily find foods that help me address my micronutrient deficiencies and maintain a balanced diet.\n\n*   **Technical Requirements:**\n    *   Algorithm to generate food recommendations based on user data (food logs, profile information, preferences).\n    *   Filtering options based on dietary restrictions (e.g., vegetarian, vegan, gluten-free).\n    *   Display of nutritional information for recommended foods.\n    *   Integration with the micronutrient analysis service to prioritize foods rich in deficient nutrients.\n\n*   **Database Changes:**\n    *   Add a `user_preferences` table to store user dietary restrictions, preferences, and allergies.\n    *   Extend the `foods` table with more detailed information about dietary suitability (e.g., `is_vegetarian`, `is_vegan`, `contains_gluten`).\n\n*   **Backend Components:**\n    *   Create a `RecommendationEngine` service that takes a user ID as input.\n    *   The `RecommendationEngine` service retrieves user data (food logs, profile, preferences) and micronutrient analysis results.\n    *   The service uses a recommendation algorithm to generate a list of personalized food recommendations, taking into account dietary restrictions, preferences, and identified deficiencies.\n    *   Create a new endpoint in the `FoodIntakeController` to retrieve food recommendations.\n\n*   **Frontend Components:**\n    *   Create `FoodRecommendations.tsx`: Displays a list of personalized food recommendations.\n    *   Create `FoodRecommendationItem.tsx`: Component to display individual food recommendations, including nutritional information and dietary suitability.\n    *   Create `FoodRecommendationFilters.tsx`: Component to allow users to filter recommendations based on dietary restrictions and preferences.\n    *   Add a button/link on the `MicronutrientReport.tsx` and/or `FoodIntakeSummary.tsx` to navigate to the `FoodRecommendations.tsx`.\n\n*   **API Endpoints:**\n    *   `GET /api/recommendations/{user_id}/foods`: Returns a JSON object containing a list of personalized food recommendations for the specified user.\n\n---\n\n**Feature 4: Supplement suggestions based on deficiencies**\n\n*   **Description:** Provide supplement suggestions based on identified micronutrient deficiencies.\n\n*   **User Story:** As a user, I want to receive supplement suggestions if I have micronutrient deficiencies, so I can consider supplementing my diet to improve my health.\n\n*   **Technical Requirements:**\n    *   Integration with the micronutrient analysis service to identify deficiencies.\n    *   A database of supplements and their micronutrient content.\n    *   Algorithm to suggest appropriate supplements based on the severity of deficiencies.\n    *   Disclaimer about consulting a healthcare professional before taking any supplements.\n\n*   **Database Changes:**\n    *   Create a `supplements` table to store information about available supplements, including their micronutrient content, dosage recommendations, and potential side effects.\n\n*   **Backend Components:**\n    *   Create a `SupplementRecommendationEngine` service that takes a user ID and a list of micronutrient deficiencies as input.\n    *   The `SupplementRecommendationEngine` service retrieves information about relevant supplements from the database.\n    *   The service generates a list of supplement suggestions based on the severity of deficiencies and considers potential interactions or contraindications (this part will require more complex logic and possibly external data sources).\n    *   Create a new endpoint in the `UserController` or a dedicated `SupplementController` to retrieve supplement recommendations.\n\n*   **Frontend Components:**\n    *   Create `SupplementRecommendations.tsx`: Displays a list of supplement suggestions.\n    *   Create `SupplementRecommendationItem.tsx`: Component to display individual supplement suggestions, including dosage information, potential side effects, and a disclaimer.\n    *   Integrate with the `MicronutrientReport.tsx` to display supplement recommendations alongside micronutrient analysis results.\n\n*   **API Endpoints:**\n    *   `GET /api/supplements/{user_id}`: Returns a JSON object containing a list of supplement recommendations for the specified user.\n\n---\n\n**Feature 5: User profile and health data management**\n\n*   **Description:** Allow users to manage their profile information, including age, gender, weight, height, activity level, and dietary preferences.\n\n*   **User Story:** As a user, I want to be able to manage my profile information (age, gender, weight, etc.) so that the system can provide personalized recommendations and accurate analysis.\n\n*   **Technical Requirements:**\n    *   Form to collect user profile data.\n    *   Validation of user input.\n    *   Secure storage of user data.\n    *   Integration with the recommendation engine and micronutrient analysis service to use profile data for personalization.\n\n*   **Database Changes:**\n    *   Add fields to the `users` table to store profile information (age, gender, weight, height, activity level, dietary preferences, allergies, etc.).\n\n*   **Backend Components:**\n    *   Create a `UserController` to handle user profile management.\n    *   Implement methods to create, read, update, and delete user profile information.\n    *   Implement validation logic for user input.\n\n*   **Frontend Components:**\n    *   Create `UserProfile.tsx`: Displays the user's profile information and allows them to edit it.\n    *   Create `UserProfileForm.tsx`: Form to collect user profile data.\n    *   Create `ProfileSettings.tsx`: Component that allows the user to manage various settings related to their profile, like privacy settings, notification preferences.\n    *   Add a link to the user profile in the application's navigation.\n\n*   **API Endpoints:**\n    *   `GET /api/users/{user_id}`: Returns a JSON object containing the user's profile information.\n    *   `PUT /api/users/{user_id}`: Updates the user's profile information.\n",
