          "raw_content": "```markdown\n# social-spark-ai Architecture\n\n## Overview\n\nAI-powered social media management for small businesses: automate content, optimize posting, and drive local engagement. Social Spark AI aims to provide a user-friendly and affordable solution that helps small businesses thrive on social media without requiring extensive marketing expertise or large budgets.\n\n### Problem Statement\n\nSmall businesses struggle to manage social media effectively, leading to inconsistent posting, low engagement, and wasted time. Many lack the resources and expertise to create compelling content, optimize posting schedules, and analyze performance data. This results in missed opportunities for brand awareness, customer acquisition, and revenue growth. Social Spark AI aims to solve this problem by providing an AI-powered platform that automates key social media tasks, optimizes content for maximum impact, and provides actionable insights to improve performance.\n\n### Target Audience\n\nSmall business owners, marketing teams with limited resources, solopreneurs wanting an easy and affordable social media solution. This includes businesses across various industries, such as retail, restaurants, service providers, and e-commerce, who are looking to build their online presence and connect with their target audience.\n\n### High-Level Goals\n\n*   **Automation:** Automate repetitive social media tasks to save time and effort.\n*   **Optimization:** Optimize content and posting schedules for maximum engagement.\n*   **Insights:** Provide actionable insights to improve social media performance.\n*   **Affordability:** Offer a cost-effective solution for small businesses.\n*   **User-Friendliness:** Design an intuitive and easy-to-use platform.\n\n## Core Entities\n\n*   **User:** Represents a user of the platform (business owner, marketing team member).  Includes attributes like email, password, company name, plan type, etc.\n*   **Account:** Represents a social media account connected to the platform (Facebook, Instagram, Twitter, LinkedIn, etc.).  Includes attributes like platform, account ID, access tokens, etc.\n*   **Content:** Represents a piece of content to be posted on social media (text, image, video, link). Includes attributes like text, media URLs, scheduled post time, etc.\n*   **Campaign:** A group of content pieces scheduled for a specific purpose.\n*   **Schedule:** Represents the schedule for posting content to social media accounts. Includes attributes like post time, frequency, recurring rules, etc.\n*   **Analytics:** Represents data collected from social media platforms (impressions, reach, engagement, clicks, etc.). Includes attributes like metric name, value, timestamp, etc.\n*   **Post:** Represents a post published to a social media account.  Includes attributes like post ID, account ID, content ID, status (success, failed), etc.\n*   **AI Model:** Configuration for an AI Model used for content generation or scheduling\n*   **Prompt:** Template for prompts passed to an AI Model\n*   **Plan:** Defines a subscription plan with feature limits and pricing.\n*   **Payment:** Tracks subscription payments and billing information.\n*   **Role:** Defines user roles (admin, user).\n*   **Permission:** Defines specific actions a role can perform.\n\n## Design System\n\n### Typography\n\n*   **Font**: Inter\n*   **Font Stack**: `'Inter', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif`\n*   **Google Fonts**: [https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Inter](https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Inter)\n*   **Rationale**: Modern, highly readable font optimized for screens. Its geometric design and variable font weights provide excellent versatility and legibility across various UI elements.\n\n### Color Palette\n\nProfessional palette with strong contrast for accessibility, adhering to WCAG AA guidelines. The colors are logically structured to represent different states and actions.\n\n*   **Primary Blue**: `#0066CC` - Primary actions and branding. Used for buttons, links, and other interactive elements.\n*   **Secondary Gray**: `#6B7280` - Secondary text and elements. Used for less important text, icons, and dividers.\n*   **Accent Orange**: `#F59E0B` - Call-to-action and highlights. Used to draw attention to important elements or actions.\n*   **Dark Gray**: `#111827` - Primary text. Used for the main body of text and headings.\n*   **Light Gray**: `#F9FAFB` - Background. Used for the main background of the application.\n*   **Border Gray**: `#E5E7EB` - Borders and dividers. Used to separate sections and elements.\n*   **Green**: `#10B981` - Success states. Used to indicate successful actions or events.\n*   **Yellow**: `#F59E0B` - Warning messages. Used to indicate potential problems or issues.\n*   **Red**: `#EF4444` - Error states. Used to indicate errors or failures.\n\n## System Architecture\n\nSocial Spark AI will utilize a multi-tiered architecture to ensure scalability, maintainability, and security. The architecture will consist of the following layers:\n\n*   **Presentation Layer (Frontend):** This layer provides the user interface for interacting with the platform.  It will be separated into admin and user portals.\n*   **Application Layer (Backend):** This layer handles business logic, data processing, and API endpoints.\n*   **Data Layer (Database):** This layer stores all application data, including user accounts, social media accounts, content, schedules, and analytics.\n*   **AI Layer:** This layer houses the AI models responsible for content generation, schedule optimization, and other intelligent features.\n*   **Integration Layer:** This layer handles communication with third-party social media platforms and other services.\n\n**Diagram:**\n\n```\n+---------------------+      +---------------------+      +---------------------+\n|  Admin Portal       |      |  User Portal        |      |  Social Media       |\n| (React/Vue/Angular) |      | (React/Vue/Angular) |      |  Platforms (API)   |\n+--------+------------+      +--------+------------+      +--------+------------+\n         |                     |                     |                     |\n         |                     |                     |                     |\n         +--------+------------+      +--------+------------+      +--------+------------+\n         |    API Gateway     |      |    API Gateway     |      |     Third-Party  |\n         |   (Load Balancer)   |      |   (Load Balancer)   |      |     Services     |\n         +--------+------------+      +--------+------------+      +--------+------------+\n                |                     |                     |                     |\n                |                     |                     |                     |\n                +---------------------+      +---------------------+      +---------------------+\n                |     Backend Services    |      |     Backend Services    |      |       AI Services     |\n                | (Authentication, User  |      | (Content Management,   |      | (Content Generation, |\n                | Management, Analytics) |      | Scheduling, Analytics) |      | Schedule Optimization) |\n                +---------------------+      +---------------------+      +---------------------+\n                                        |                     |\n                                        |                     |\n                                        +---------------------+\n                                        |      Database       |\n                                        |   (PostgreSQL/MySQL)  |\n                                        +---------------------+\n```\n\n**Admin Portal:**\n\n*   Access to user management, subscription management, feature configuration, and system monitoring.\n\n**User Portal:**\n\n*   Access to social media account management, content creation, scheduling, analytics, and AI-powered features.\n\n## Database Schema\n\nWe'll use a relational database (e.g., PostgreSQL or MySQL) to store application data.  The schema will include tables for core entities and RBAC.\n\n**Key Tables:**\n\n*   `users`: Stores user information (id, email, password, company_name, plan_id, created_at, updated_at).\n*   `accounts`: Stores social media account information (id, user_id, platform, account_id, access_token, created_at, updated_at).\n*   `contents`: Stores content information (id, user_id, content_type, text, media_url, created_at, updated_at).\n*   `campaigns`: Stores campaigns information (id, user_id, name, description, created_at, updated_at).\n*   `schedules`: Stores schedule information (id, content_id, account_id, post_time, recurring_rule, created_at, updated_at).\n*   `posts`: Stores post information (id, content_id, account_id, post_id, status, created_at, updated_at).\n*   `analytics`: Stores analytics data (id, account_id, metric_name, value, timestamp).\n*   `ai_models`: Stores AI model configurations (id, name, model_type, settings, created_at, updated_at).\n*   `prompts`: Stores prompt templates (id, ai_model_id, prompt_text, created_at, updated_at).\n*   `plans`: Stores subscription plan information (id, name, price, feature_limits, created_at, updated_at).\n*   `payments`: Stores payment information (id, user_id, plan_id, amount, payment_date, created_at, updated_at).\n\n**RBAC Tables:**\n\n*   `roles`: Stores roles (id, name). Example: admin, user\n*   `permissions`: Stores permissions (id, name). Example: create_post, edit_user, view_analytics\n*   `role_permissions`: Maps roles to permissions (role_id, permission_id).\n*   `user_roles`: Maps users to roles (user_id, role_id).\n\n**ER Diagram (Simplified):**\n\n```\n+----------+      +----------+      +----------+      +----------+      +----------+\n|  users   |----->|  accounts |----->|  contents |----->| schedules|----->|  posts   |\n+----------+      +----------+      +----------+      +----------+      +----------+\n     ^               ^               ^\n     |               |               |\n     +---------------+---------------+\n                     |\n                     v\n             +----------+\n             | analytics|\n             +----------+\n\n+----------+      +----------+\n|  ai_models|----->|  prompts |\n+----------+      +----------+\n\n+----------+      +----------+      +----------+\n|  plans   |<-----|   users  |----->| payments |\n+----------+      +----------+\n\n+----------+      +-----------------+      +------------------+\n|  roles   |----->|   role_permissions|<-----|    permissions   |\n+----------+      +-----------------+      +------------------+\n     ^\n     |\n     +----------+\n     | user_roles|\n     +----------+\n```\n\n## Comprehensive API Design\n\nThe API will be a RESTful API using JSON for data exchange.  It will be secured using JWT (JSON Web Tokens).\n\n**General API Structure:**\n\n*   Base URL: `/api/v1/`\n*   Authentication:  `Authorization: Bearer <JWT_TOKEN>` header required for most endpoints.\n\n**Admin Endpoints (Examples - 20+):**\n\n*   `GET /api/v1/admin/users`: List all users.\n*   `GET /api/v1/admin/users/{user_id}`: Get a specific user.\n*   `POST /api/v1/admin/users`: Create a new user.\n*   `PUT /api/v1/admin/users/{user_id}`: Update a user.\n*   `DELETE /api/v1/admin/users/{user_id}`: Delete a user.\n*   `GET /api/v1/admin/plans`: List all subscription plans.\n*   `POST /api/v1/admin/plans`: Create a new subscription plan.\n*   `PUT /api/v1/admin/plans/{plan_id}`: Update a subscription plan.\n*   `DELETE /api/v1/admin/plans/{plan_id}`: Delete a subscription plan.\n*   `GET /api/v1/admin/analytics`: Get system-wide analytics.\n*   `GET /api/v1/admin/roles`: List all roles.\n*   `POST /api/v1/admin/roles`: Create a new role.\n*   `PUT /api/v1/admin/roles/{role_id}`: Update a role.\n*   `DELETE /api/v1/admin/roles/{role_id}`: Delete a role.\n*   `GET /api/v1/admin/permissions`: List all permissions.\n*   `POST /api/v1/admin/permissions`: Create a new permission.\n*   `PUT /api/v1/admin/permissions/{permission_id}`: Update a permission.\n*   `DELETE /api/v1/admin/permissions/{permission_id}`: Delete a permission.\n*   `GET /api/v1/admin/ai-models`: List all AI Models.\n*   `POST /api/v1/admin/ai-models`: Create a new AI Model.\n*   `PUT /api/v1/admin/ai-models/{ai_model_id}`: Update an AI Model.\n*   `DELETE /api/v1/admin/ai-models/{ai_model_id}`: Delete an AI Model.\n*   `GET /api/v1/admin/system-status`: Get system status information.\n\n**User Endpoints (Examples - 20+):**\n\n*   `POST /api/v1/auth/register`: Register a new user account.\n*   `POST /api/v1/auth/login`: Log in and obtain a JWT token.\n*   `GET /api/v1/auth/me`: Get the current user's profile.\n*   `PUT /api/v1/auth/me`: Update the current user's profile.\n*   `POST /api/v1/accounts`: Create a new social media account connection.\n*   `GET /api/v1/accounts`: List all connected social media accounts.\n*   `GET /api/v1/accounts/{account_id}`: Get a specific social media account.\n*   `PUT /api/v1/accounts/{account_id}`: Update a social media account.\n*   `DELETE /api/v1/accounts/{account_id}`: Delete a social media account.\n*   `POST /api/v1/contents`: Create a new content item.\n*   `GET /api/v1/contents`: List all content items.\n*   `GET /api/v1/contents/{content_id}`: Get a specific content item.\n*   `PUT /api/v1/contents/{content_id}`: Update a content item.\n*   `DELETE /api/v1/contents/{content_id}`: Delete a content item.\n*   `POST /api/v1/schedules`: Create a new schedule.\n*   `GET /api/v1/schedules`: List all schedules.\n*   `GET /api/v1/schedules/{schedule_id}`: Get a specific schedule.\n*   `PUT /api/v1/schedules/{schedule_id}`: Update a schedule.\n*   `DELETE /api/v1/schedules/{schedule_id}`: Delete a schedule.\n*   `GET /api/v1/analytics`: Get analytics data for connected accounts.\n*    `POST /api/v1/ai/generate-content`: Generates social media content with AI.\n*   `GET /api/v1/plans`: List available plans.\n*   `POST /api/v1/payments/subscribe`: Subscribe to a specific plan.\n\n**API Documentation:**\n\n*   We'll use OpenAPI (Swagger) to document the API.\n\n## Frontend Structure\n\nThe frontend will be built using a modern JavaScript framework (e.g., React, Vue, or Angular).  It will be structured into two separate portals: an admin portal and a user portal.\n\n**Admin Portal:**\n\n*   Provides access to administrative features, such as user management, plan management, and system configuration.\n*   Will likely be more complex and require advanced UI components.\n\n**User Portal:**\n\n*   Provides access to user-facing features, such as social media account management, content creation, scheduling, and analytics.\n*   Emphasis on user-friendliness and ease of use.\n\n**Common Components:**\n\n*   Authentication components (login, registration, password reset).\n*   Navigation components (menus, breadcrumbs).\n*   Data display components (tables, charts).\n*   Form components (input fields, dropdowns, buttons).\n\n**Directory Structure (Example using React):**\n\n```\nfrontend/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 src/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 components/       # Reusable UI components\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Auth/        # Authentication components\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Navigation/   # Navigation components\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 DataDisplay/ # Data Display components\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Forms/      # Form components\n\u2502   \u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 ...\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 pages/          # Pages for both admin and user portals\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Admin/       # Admin portal pages\n\u2502   \u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Users/\n\u2502   \u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Plans/\n\u2502   \u2502   \u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 ...\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 User/        # User portal pages\n\u2502   \u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Dashboard/\n\u2502   \u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Accounts/\n\u2502   \u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Content/\n\u2502   \u2502   \u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 ...\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 App.js           # Main application component\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 index.js         # Entry point\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 ...\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 ...\n```\n\n## Real-time & Events Architecture\n\nReal-time updates and event handling will be implemented using WebSockets or Server-Sent Events (SSE). This will allow for features like:\n\n*   **Live analytics dashboards:**  Displaying real-time updates on social media engagement.\n*   **Notifications:**  Alerting users about important events (e.g., post published successfully, account connection failed).\n*   **Content updates:** Displaying content changes in real-time to multiple users.\n\n**Implementation:**\n\n*   A dedicated real-time server (e.g., using Node.js with Socket.IO or a dedicated SSE server) will handle WebSocket/SSE connections.\n*   The backend services will publish events to a message queue (e.g., RabbitMQ or Kafka).\n*   The real-time server will subscribe to the message queue and push events to connected clients.\n\n**Diagram:**\n\n```\n+-----------------+    +-----------------+    +-----------------+\n|   Frontend      |    | Real-time Server|    |  Backend Services|\n| (WebSocket/SSE)|<--->|  (Socket.IO)   |<--->| (Publish Events)|\n+-----------------+    +-----------------+    +--------+--------+\n                                                            |\n                                                            v\n                                                +-----------------+\n                                                | Message Queue   |\n                                                | (RabbitMQ/Kafka)|\n                                                +-----------------+\n```\n\n## Authentication & Authorization with RBAC\n\nAuthentication and authorization will be implemented using JWT (JSON Web Tokens) and Role-Based Access Control (RBAC).\n\n**Authentication:**\n\n*   Users will authenticate using email and password.\n*   Upon successful authentication, the backend will issue a JWT token.\n*   The JWT token will be stored in the frontend (e.g., in localStorage or a cookie).\n*   Subsequent requests to the API will include the JWT token in the `Authorization` header.\n\n**Authorization (RBAC):**\n\n*   Each user will be assigned one or more roles (e.g., admin, user).\n*   Each role will be associated with a set of permissions (e.g., create_post, edit_user, view_analytics).\n*   The backend will verify that the user has the necessary permissions to perform a given action before granting access.\n*   The JWT token will contain information about the user's roles and permissions.\n\n**Implementation:**\n\n*   The backend will use a library like `jsonwebtoken` (Node.js) or similar libraries in other languages to generate and verify JWT tokens.\n*   RBAC will be implemented using the `roles` , `permissions` , `role_permissions` , and `user_roles` database tables.\n\n## Deployment & Scalability\n\nThe application will be deployed to a cloud platform (e.g., AWS, Google Cloud, or Azure).\n\n**Deployment:**\n\n*   The frontend will be deployed to a static hosting service (e.g., AWS S3, Google Cloud Storage, or Netlify).\n*   The backend services will be deployed to a container orchestration platform (e.g., Kubernetes or Docker Swarm).\n*   The database will be deployed to a managed database service (e.g., AWS RDS, Google Cloud SQL, or Azure SQL Database).\n\n**Scalability:**\n\n*   The backend services will be scaled horizontally by adding more containers.\n*   A load balancer will distribute traffic across the backend containers.\n*   The database will be scaled vertically by increasing the resources allocated to the database server.\n*   Caching will be used to reduce the load on the database.\n\n**Diagram:**\n\n```\n+-----------------+      +-----------------+      +-----------------+\n|   Load Balancer  |----->| Backend Services|----->|      Database   |\n+-----------------+      +-----------------+      +-----------------+\n                        | (Kubernetes/Docker)|      | (Managed Service)|\n                        +-----------------+      +-----------------+\n```\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\nA comprehensive testing strategy will be implemented to ensure the quality and reliability of the application.  This will include:\n\n*   **Unit Tests:** Testing individual components and functions in isolation.\n*   **Integration Tests:** Testing the interaction between different components and services.\n*   **End-to-End Tests:** Testing the entire application flow from the user interface to the database.\n*   **Performance Tests:** Testing the application's performance under load.\n*   **Security Tests:** Testing the application for security vulnerabilities.\n\n**Tools:**\n\n*   Jest, Mocha, Cypress, Selenium, JMeter\n\n## Security & Hardening\n\nSecurity is a top priority.  We will implement various security measures to protect the application and its data.\n\n*   **Authentication and Authorization:** Securely authenticate and authorize users using JWT and RBAC.\n*   **Input Validation:** Validate all user inputs to prevent injection attacks (e.g., SQL injection, XSS).\n*   **Output Encoding:** Encode all user outputs to prevent XSS attacks.\n*   **HTTPS:** Use HTTPS to encrypt all communication between the client and the server.\n*   **Regular Security Audits:** Conduct regular security audits to identify and fix vulnerabilities.\n*   **Dependency Management:**  Keep dependencies up to date to patch security vulnerabilities.\n*   **Rate Limiting:** Implement rate limiting to prevent brute-force attacks.\n*   **Data Encryption:** Encrypt sensitive data at rest and in transit.\n*   **OWASP Compliance:** Adhere to OWASP (Open Web Application Security Project) guidelines.\n\n## Logging & Observability\n\nComprehensive logging and observability will be implemented to monitor the application's health and performance.\n\n*   **Centralized Logging:**  Log all application events to a central logging system (e.g., ELK stack or Splunk).\n*   **Metrics Monitoring:**  Monitor key performance metrics (e.g., CPU usage, memory usage, response time) using a monitoring tool (e.g., Prometheus or Grafana).\n*   **Alerting:**  Set up alerts to notify administrators when critical events occur (e.g., high error rate, low disk space).\n*   **Tracing:** Implement distributed tracing to track requests across different services.\n\n**Implementation:**\n\n*   Use a logging library (e.g., Winston or Log4j) to log application events.\n*   Use a metrics monitoring tool (e.g., Prometheus or Grafana) to collect and visualize metrics.\n*   Use a tracing tool (e.g., Jaeger or Zipkin) to implement distributed tracing.\n\n## Features Overview\n\nSocial Spark AI aims to provide a comprehensive suite of features for managing social media effectively. The features are categorized below:\n\n**Core Functionality:**\n\n1.  **Social Media Account Management:** Connect and manage multiple social media accounts (Facebook, Instagram, Twitter, LinkedIn, etc.).\n2.  **Content Creation:**  Create and edit social media posts with text, images, and videos.\n3.  **Content Scheduling:** Schedule posts to be published at specific times and dates.\n4.  **Campaign Management:** Group content into campaigns for specific marketing goals.\n5.  **Analytics Tracking:** Track key performance metrics such as impressions, reach, engagement, and clicks.\n6.  **User Roles and Permissions:**  Control access to features based on user roles (admin, user).\n7.  **Notifications:**  Receive notifications about important events (e.g., post published, account connection error).\n8.  **Dashboard:** View a summary of key metrics and recent activity.\n9.  **Calendar View:** Visualize scheduled posts in a calendar format.\n10. **Team Collaboration:** Allow multiple team members to collaborate on social media management.\n11. **Reporting:** Generate reports on social media performance.\n12. **Mobile Accessibility:** Access and manage social media on mobile devices.\n\n**AI-Powered Features:**\n\n13. **AI Content Generation:**  Generate social media post ideas and text using AI.\n14. **AI Schedule Optimization:**  Optimize posting schedules for maximum engagement using AI.\n15. **AI Hashtag Recommendation:** Suggest relevant hashtags to increase reach using AI.\n16. **AI Image Enhancement:** Improve the quality of images using AI.\n17. **AI Tone Analysis:** Analyze the tone of social media posts and suggest improvements.\n18. **AI Competitor Analysis:** Analyze competitor social media activity.\n\n**Content Management & Creation:**\n\n19. **Content Library:** Store and organize social media content in a library.\n20. **Image Editing:**  Edit images directly within the platform.\n21. **Video Upload & Hosting:** Upload and host videos for social media posts.\n22. **Link Shortening:** Shorten long URLs for social media posts.\n23. **Content Recycling:** Re-use existing content in new social media posts.\n\n**Scheduling & Automation:**\n\n24. **Recurring Posts:** Schedule posts to be published on a recurring basis.\n25. **Bulk Scheduling:** Schedule multiple posts at once.\n26. **Auto-Posting:** Automatically publish content from RSS feeds or other sources.\n27. **Time Zone Support:**  Schedule posts based on different time zones.\n\n**Engagement & Community Management:**\n\n28. **Social Listening:** Monitor social media for mentions of your brand or keywords.\n29. **Comment Management:** Respond to comments and messages directly from the platform.\n30. **Direct Messaging:** Manage direct messages from social media accounts.\n31. **Sentiment Analysis:** Analyze the sentiment of comments and messages.\n\n**Additional Features:**\n\n32. **Integration with CRM Systems:** Integrate with popular CRM systems to track leads and customers.\n33. **Integration with E-commerce Platforms:** Integrate with e-commerce platforms to promote products and services.\n34. **Multilingual Support:** Support multiple languages for international audiences.\n35. **Custom Branding:** Customize the platform with your brand colors and logo.\n36. **Two-Factor Authentication:** Enhance security with two-factor authentication.\n37. **API Access:** Provide API access for developers to integrate with other applications.\n38. **White Labeling:** Offer a white-labeled version of the platform for agencies and resellers.\n39. **Content Curation:**  Suggest relevant articles and content from third-party sources for sharing.\n40. **A/B Testing:** Test different versions of social media posts to optimize performance.\n\n## Feature Implementation Roadmap\n\nHere are the 5 most important features for `social-spark-ai`, along with their detailed specifications, implementation roadmap, and database changes.\n\n### 1. AI-Powered Content Generation\n**Description:** Generates social media content based on user-defined keywords, industry, and target audience. This feature helps small businesses create engaging and relevant content quickly and efficiently.\n\n**User Story:** As a small business owner, I want to input keywords related to my business and have the AI generate suggested social media posts so that I can save time on content creation.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n*   Integrate with a natural language generation (NLG) AI model (e.g., OpenAI's GPT models or similar).\n*   Develop a content recommendation algorithm based on user input and industry trends.\n*   Implement a content preview and editing interface.\n*   Implement profanity and content moderation filters.\n\n**Database Changes:**\n*   **`users` table:** Add `industry` (string) and `target_audience` (string) fields.\n*   **`generated_content` table:** `id` (INT, PK, AI), `user_id` (INT, FK to `users.id`), `content` (TEXT), `platform` (ENUM: \"Facebook\", \"Twitter\", \"Instagram\", \"LinkedIn\"), `created_at` (TIMESTAMP), `keywords` (TEXT).\n\n**Backend Components:**\n*   **Model:** `GeneratedContent.js` (defines structure and relations for generated content)\n*   **Service:** `ContentGenerationService.js` (handles API calls to the AI model, content filtering, and database interaction).\n*   **Controller:** `ContentGenerationController.js` (handles HTTP requests for content generation).\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n*   `ContentGenerator.tsx`: Form for inputting keywords and generating content suggestions.\n*   `ContentPreview.tsx`: Displays the generated content for review and editing.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n*   `POST /api/content/generate`: Generates social media content based on user input.\n*   `GET /api/content/history`: Returns a history of generated content for the user.\n\n### 2. Automated Post Scheduling\n**Description:** Allows users to schedule social media posts across multiple platforms at optimal times. This feature ensures consistent posting and maximizes audience reach.\n\n**User Story:** As a marketing team member, I want to schedule posts for different platforms in advance so that I can maintain a consistent social media presence even when I'm busy.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n*   Integrate with social media platform APIs (Facebook, Twitter, Instagram, LinkedIn, etc.).\n*   Develop a scheduling algorithm that optimizes posting times based on audience activity.\n*   Implement a calendar view for managing scheduled posts.\n*   Implement OAuth authentication for each social media platform.\n\n**Database Changes:**\n*   **`scheduled_posts` table:** `id` (INT, PK, AI), `user_id` (INT, FK to `users.id`), `platform` (ENUM: \"Facebook\", \"Twitter\", \"Instagram\", \"LinkedIn\"), `content` (TEXT), `scheduled_time` (TIMESTAMP), `status` (ENUM: \"scheduled\", \"posted\", \"failed\"), `post_id` (TEXT - platform's ID if posted).\n\n**Backend Components:**\n*   **Model:** `ScheduledPost.js` (defines structure and relations for scheduled posts).\n*   **Service:** `SchedulingService.js` (handles post scheduling, API calls to social media platforms, and status updates).\n*   **Controller:** `SchedulingController.js` (handles HTTP requests for managing scheduled posts).\n*   **Worker:** `PostSchedulerWorker.js` (runs periodically to check for posts ready to be published).\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n*   `SchedulerCalendar.tsx`: Displays a calendar view for scheduling posts.\n*   `PostEditor.tsx`: Allows users to create and edit social media posts.\n*   `ScheduledPostsList.tsx`: List of scheduled posts with actions to edit, delete, or reschedule.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n*   `POST /api/posts/schedule`: Schedules a social media post.\n*   `GET /api/posts/scheduled`: Retrieves a list of scheduled posts.\n*   `PUT /api/posts/schedule/:id`: Updates a scheduled post.\n*   `DELETE /api/posts/schedule/:id`: Deletes a scheduled post.\n\n### 3. Engagement Tracking and Analytics\n**Description:** Provides insights into social media performance, including likes, shares, comments, and follower growth. This feature allows users to track their progress and optimize their social media strategy.\n\n**User Story:** As a small business owner, I want to see analytics on my social media posts so that I can understand what content performs best and optimize my strategy accordingly.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n*   Retrieve metrics from social media platform APIs (Facebook Insights, Twitter API, Instagram API, LinkedIn Analytics).\n*   Develop data visualization tools to display key metrics.\n*   Implement reporting functionality to generate custom reports.\n\n**Database Changes:**\n*   **`post_metrics` table:** `id` (INT, PK, AI), `post_id` (TEXT, indexed), `platform` (ENUM: \"Facebook\", \"Twitter\", \"Instagram\", \"LinkedIn\"), `likes` (INT), `shares` (INT), `comments` (INT), `reach` (INT), `impressions` (INT), `date` (DATE).\n*   **`user_metrics` table:** `id` (INT, PK, AI), `user_id` (INT, FK to `users.id`), `platform` (ENUM: \"Facebook\", \"Twitter\", \"Instagram\", \"LinkedIn\"), `followers` (INT), `date` (DATE).\n\n**Backend Components:**\n*   **Model:** `PostMetric.js`, `UserMetric.js` (defines structure for metrics).\n*   **Service:** `AnalyticsService.js` (retrieves metrics from social media platforms, processes, and stores data).\n*   **Controller:** `AnalyticsController.js` (handles HTTP requests for retrieving analytics data).\n*   **Worker:** `MetricsCollectorWorker.js` (periodically collects metrics from social media platforms).\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n*   `Dashboard.tsx`: Displays an overview of social media performance metrics.\n*   `PostAnalytics.tsx`: Detailed analytics for individual posts.\n*   `FollowerGrowthChart.tsx`: Visualizes follower growth over time.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n*   `GET /api/analytics/posts/:platform`: Retrieves analytics for all posts on a specific platform.\n*   `GET /api/analytics/posts/:platform/:post_id`: Retrieves analytics for a specific post.\n*   `GET /api/analytics/followers/:platform`: Retrieves follower growth analytics for a specific platform.\n\n### 4. Local Engagement Tools\n**Description:** Helps businesses connect with local audiences by identifying relevant hashtags, events, and influencers. It optimizes content for local search and engagement.\n\n**User Story:** As a local business owner, I want to identify popular local hashtags and events to participate in so that I can increase my visibility in the local community.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n*   Integrate with location-based APIs (e.g., Google Places API, Yelp API).\n*   Develop a hashtag recommendation engine based on location and industry.\n*   Implement a feature to identify local events and influencers.\n*   Implement location tagging for posts.\n\n**Database Changes:**\n*   **`location_hashtags` table:** `id` (INT, PK, AI), `location` (STRING), `hashtag` (STRING), `trend_score` (INT).\n*   **`local_events` table:** `id` (INT, PK, AI), `location` (STRING), `event_name` (STRING), `event_date` (TIMESTAMP), `description` (TEXT).\n*   **`users` table:** Add `location` (string) field.\n\n**Backend Components:**\n*   **Model:** `LocationHashtag.js`, `LocalEvent.js`.\n*   **Service:** `LocalEngagementService.js` (handles location-based API calls, hashtag recommendation, and event identification).\n*   **Controller:** `LocalEngagementController.js` (handles HTTP requests for local engagement tools).\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n*   `LocalHashtagSuggestions.tsx`: Displays recommended local hashtags.\n*   `LocalEventFinder.tsx`: Allows users to search for local events.\n*   `LocationTagging.tsx`: Enables location tagging for posts.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n*   `GET /api/local/hashtags`: Retrieves local hashtag suggestions based on location.\n*   `GET /api/local/events`: Retrieves local events based on location.\n\n### 5. Social Media Listening and Brand Monitoring\n**Description:** Monitors social media for mentions of the business, its products, or related keywords. It allows users to track brand sentiment, identify customer feedback, and respond to inquiries or complaints in real-time.\n\n**User Story:** As a marketing team, I want to monitor social media for mentions of my brand so that I can track customer sentiment and address any issues or feedback promptly.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n*   Integrate with social media listening APIs (e.g., Brandwatch, Mention, or social media platform APIs themselves).\n*   Implement sentiment analysis to determine the tone of mentions.\n*   Develop an alert system to notify users of critical mentions or sentiment changes.\n\n**Database Changes:**\n*   **`brand_mentions` table:** `id` (INT, PK, AI), `platform` (ENUM: \"Facebook\", \"Twitter\", \"Instagram\", \"LinkedIn\"), `mention_text` (TEXT), `mention_url` (TEXT), `mention_date` (TIMESTAMP), `sentiment` (ENUM: \"positive\", \"negative\", \"neutral\"), `user_id` (INT, FK to `users.id`).\n*   **`monitored_keywords` table:** `id` (INT, PK, AI), `user_id` (INT, FK to `users.id`), `keyword` (STRING).\n\n**Backend Components:**\n*   **Model:** `BrandMention.js`, `MonitoredKeyword.js`.\n*   **Service:** `SocialListeningService.js` (handles API calls to social listening platforms, sentiment analysis, and database interaction).\n*   **Controller:** `SocialListeningController.js` (handles HTTP requests for brand monitoring).\n*   **Worker:** `MentionCollectorWorker.js` (periodically collects mentions and performs sentiment analysis).\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n*   `MentionDashboard.tsx`: Displays a dashboard of brand mentions.\n*   `SentimentAnalysisChart.tsx`: Visualizes brand sentiment over time.\n*   `KeywordManagement.tsx`: Allows users to manage monitored keywords.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n*   `GET /api/monitoring/mentions`: Retrieves a list of brand mentions.\n*   `GET /api/monitoring/sentiment`: Retrieves sentiment analysis data.\n*   `POST /api/monitoring/keywords`: Adds a new keyword to monitor.\n*   `DELETE /api/monitoring/keywords/:id`: Deletes a monitored keyword.\n\nThis detailed breakdown provides a solid foundation for implementing `social-spark-ai` and addressing the core needs of its target audience.\n",
