          "raw_content": "```markdown\n# ai-text-summarizer Architecture\n\n## Overview\n\nAI-powered tool to summarize text, audio, and video, saving users time and improving comprehension. Perfect for students & professionals. This document outlines the architecture of the AI Text Summarizer application, detailing the system design, data model, API structure, and other critical aspects. The application provides functionality for users to upload text, audio, or video content and receive a concise summary generated by AI models. It caters to both individual users and administrative staff.\n\n### Problem Statement\n\nStudents and professionals struggle to quickly summarize large amounts of information (articles, papers, books) efficiently for work and study. The manual summarization process is time-consuming, cognitively demanding, and often leads to inconsistencies in the quality and style of the summary. Furthermore, extracting key insights from audio and video content presents additional challenges due to the need for transcription and subsequent analysis.\n\n### Target Audience\n\nStudents (high school, college, graduate) needing to condense study materials; Researchers analyzing documents; Professionals staying current in their fields. Specifically, the target audience includes:\n\n*   **Students:** High school, college, and graduate students needing to quickly grasp the main points of academic papers, textbooks, and research articles.\n*   **Researchers:** Individuals involved in research who need to efficiently analyze large volumes of documents to identify relevant information.\n*   **Professionals:** Professionals who need to stay updated on industry news, reports, and publications but lack the time to read everything in detail.\n*   **Journalists:** Reporters and editors looking to quickly summarize news articles, press releases, and other sources of information.\n*   **Legal Professionals:** Lawyers and paralegals needing to summarize legal documents, case files, and transcripts.\n\n### Goals\n\n*   Provide accurate and concise summaries of text, audio, and video content.\n*   Offer a user-friendly interface that is accessible to users with varying levels of technical expertise.\n*   Support multiple languages and formats.\n*   Ensure the security and privacy of user data.\n*   Enable administrators to manage user accounts, content, and system settings.\n*   Provide a scalable and reliable platform that can handle a large volume of requests.\n\n## Core Entities\n\nThis section defines the key entities within the system and their relationships.\n\n*   **User:** Represents a registered user of the system. Contains attributes such as user ID, name, email, password, role (admin or user), and account status.\n*   **Content:** Represents the text, audio, or video content to be summarized. Contains attributes such as content ID, user ID, title, type (text, audio, video), source URL, and upload date.\n*   **Summary:** Represents the generated summary of a piece of content. Contains attributes such as summary ID, content ID, text, creation date, and model used.\n*   **Model:** Represents the AI model used for summarization. Contains attributes such as model ID, name, description, and version.\n*   **Role:** Defines the permissions associated with a user role (e.g., admin, user).\n*   **Permission:** Defines a specific action or access right that can be granted to a role.\n\n## Design System\n\nThis section outlines the design principles and components used to create a consistent and visually appealing user interface.\n\n### Typography\n\n*   **Font**: Inter\n*   **Font Stack**: `'Inter', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif`\n*   **Google Fonts**: https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Inter\n*   **Rationale**: Modern, highly readable font optimized for screens. Inter provides excellent clarity at various sizes and weights, ensuring a comfortable reading experience. It also offers a wide range of weights, enabling us to create a clear visual hierarchy.\n\n### Color Palette\n\nProfessional palette with strong contrast for accessibility. Complies with WCAG AA guidelines for color contrast ratios. Uses a combination of blues, grays, and orange to create a professional and inviting interface. Avoids harsh color combinations and ensures readability for users with visual impairments.\n\n*   **Primary Blue**: `#0066CC` - Primary actions and branding. Used for buttons, links, and the main brand color.\n*   **Secondary Gray**: `#6B7280` - Secondary text and elements. Used for less important text, icons, and backgrounds.\n*   **Accent Orange**: `#F59E0B` - Call-to-action and highlights. Used for important buttons, warnings, and highlights.\n*   **Dark Gray**: `#111827` - Primary text. Used for the main body of text to ensure readability.\n*   **Light Gray**: `#F9FAFB` - Background. Used for the main background to provide a clean and neutral canvas.\n*   **Border Gray**: `#E5E7EB` - Borders and dividers. Used for borders and dividers to create visual separation.\n*   **Green**: `#10B981` - Success states. Used to indicate successful actions.\n*   **Yellow**: `#F59E0B` - Warning messages. Used to indicate warnings or potential problems.\n*   **Red**: `#EF4444` - Error states. Used to indicate errors or critical problems.\n\n## System Architecture\n\nThe system adopts a multi-tier architecture comprising the following layers:\n\n*   **Presentation Layer (Frontend):** User interface for interacting with the application. Separate portals for admins and regular users. Built using React.js.\n*   **API Layer (Backend):** Provides RESTful APIs for the frontend to interact with the application logic. Built using Node.js with Express.js.\n*   **Application Layer (Business Logic):** Implements the core business logic for summarization, user management, and content management. Written in Node.js.\n*   **Data Layer (Database):** Stores all application data, including user information, content, summaries, and model information. PostgreSQL is used as the database.\n*   **AI Model Layer:** Contains the AI models used for text, audio, and video summarization. This layer interacts with the application layer through a defined interface. Deployed as separate microservices.\n*   **Infrastructure Layer:** Underlying infrastructure including cloud platform, servers, network and security components.\n\n**Admin/User Separation:**\n\n*   The application features separate frontends for administrators and regular users.\n*   The admin frontend allows administrators to manage users, content, models, and system settings.\n*   The user frontend allows users to upload content, view summaries, and manage their account.\n*   The API layer enforces access control based on user roles to ensure that only authorized users can access specific endpoints.\n\n## Database Schema\n\nThe database schema consists of the following tables:\n\n*   **users:** Stores user information (id, name, email, password, created_at, updated_at).\n*   **contents:** Stores content information (id, user_id, title, type, source_url, upload_date, created_at, updated_at).\n*   **summaries:** Stores summary information (id, content_id, text, creation_date, model_id, created_at, updated_at).\n*   **models:** Stores information about the available AI models (id, name, description, version, created_at, updated_at).\n*   **roles:** Stores roles information (id, name, description, created_at, updated_at).  e.g., 'admin', 'user'.\n*   **permissions:** Stores permissions information (id, name, description, created_at, updated_at). e.g., 'create_user', 'read_summary', 'update_model'.\n*   **role_permissions:** Stores the many-to-many relationship between roles and permissions (role_id, permission_id).\n*   **user_roles:** Stores the many-to-many relationship between users and roles (user_id, role_id).\n\n**RBAC Tables:**\n\n*   **roles:** Defines roles within the system (e.g., admin, user, moderator). Each role has a unique ID, name, and description.\n*   **permissions:** Defines specific permissions that can be granted to roles (e.g., create_user, edit_content, delete_summary). Each permission has a unique ID, name, and description.\n*   **role_permissions:** A junction table that associates roles with permissions. Each row in this table specifies which permissions are granted to a particular role.\n*   **user_roles:** A junction table that associates users with roles. Each row in this table specifies which roles are assigned to a particular user.\n\n## Comprehensive API Design\n\nThe API is designed using RESTful principles and provides endpoints for managing users, content, summaries, and models. Authentication is handled using JWT (JSON Web Tokens). Error responses follow a consistent format with appropriate HTTP status codes and error messages.\n\n**Admin Endpoints (20+):**\n\n*   `/admin/users` (GET, POST): Manage user accounts (list, create).\n*   `/admin/users/:id` (GET, PUT, DELETE): Manage specific user (retrieve, update, delete).\n*   `/admin/roles` (GET, POST): Manage roles (list, create).\n*   `/admin/roles/:id` (GET, PUT, DELETE): Manage specific role (retrieve, update, delete).\n*   `/admin/permissions` (GET, POST): Manage permissions (list, create).\n*   `/admin/permissions/:id` (GET, PUT, DELETE): Manage specific permission (retrieve, update, delete).\n*   `/admin/models` (GET, POST): Manage AI models (list, create).\n*   `/admin/models/:id` (GET, PUT, DELETE): Manage specific AI model (retrieve, update, delete).\n*   `/admin/contents` (GET): List all content.\n*   `/admin/contents/:id` (GET, DELETE): Retrieve/delete specific content.\n*   `/admin/summaries` (GET): List all summaries.\n*   `/admin/summaries/:id` (GET, DELETE): Retrieve/delete specific summary.\n*   `/admin/statistics` (GET): Get system statistics (user count, content count, summary count).\n*   `/admin/settings` (GET, PUT): Get/update system settings.\n*   `/admin/health` (GET): Check system health.\n*   `/admin/logs` (GET): View system logs.\n*   `/admin/jobs` (GET): Manage background jobs.\n*   `/admin/jobs/:id` (GET, PUT, DELETE): Manage specific job (retrieve, update, delete).\n*   `/admin/dashboard` (GET): Get dashboard stats.\n*   `/admin/audit` (GET): View system audit logs.\n\n**User Endpoints (20+):**\n\n*   `/users/register` (POST): Register a new user.\n*   `/users/login` (POST): Login a user.\n*   `/users/profile` (GET, PUT): Get/update user profile.\n*   `/users/password` (PUT): Change user password.\n*   `/users/logout` (POST): Logout a user.\n*   `/contents` (POST, GET): Upload new content, List user content.\n*   `/contents/:id` (GET, PUT, DELETE): Retrieve/update/delete specific content.\n*   `/contents/:id/summarize` (POST): Request a summary for a content.\n*   `/summaries` (GET): List user summaries.\n*   `/summaries/:id` (GET): Retrieve a specific summary.\n*   `/summaries/:id/feedback` (POST): Provide feedback on a summary.\n*   `/models` (GET): List available AI models.\n*   `/models/:id` (GET): Retrieve information about a specific AI model.\n*   `/search` (GET): Search contents and summaries.\n*   `/history` (GET): View user's history.\n*   `/settings` (GET, PUT): Get/Update user settings.\n*   `/notifications` (GET): Get user notifications.\n*   `/support` (POST): Contact support.\n*   `/faq` (GET): Retrieve FAQs.\n\n## Frontend Structure\n\nThe frontend is implemented using React.js and follows a component-based architecture. The application is divided into two main portals:\n\n*   **User Portal:** Allows users to upload content, view summaries, manage their account, and access support resources. Features include a dashboard, content library, summary view, settings page, and feedback form.\n*   **Admin Portal:** Allows administrators to manage users, content, models, system settings, and monitor system performance. Features include a user management dashboard, content moderation tools, model management interface, system configuration panel, and logging tools.\n\nThe frontend structure consists of the following directories:\n\n*   `src/components`: Reusable UI components (e.g., buttons, forms, modals, tables).\n*   `src/pages`: Pages for different routes in the application (e.g., home, login, register, dashboard).\n*   `src/services`: API client for interacting with the backend API.\n*   `src/context`: Context providers for managing global state (e.g., user authentication, theme).\n*   `src/utils`: Utility functions and helper methods.\n*   `src/styles`: Global styles and CSS modules.\n\n## Real-time & Events Architecture\n\nThe system incorporates real-time capabilities and event-driven architecture to provide a more responsive and interactive user experience. This involves technologies for managing background jobs, sending notifications, and handling asynchronous operations.\n\n*   **Background Jobs:** Summarization tasks, especially for audio and video, are processed as background jobs using a message queue system such as RabbitMQ or Kafka. This ensures that the main application thread remains responsive.\n*   **WebSockets:** WebSockets are used to provide real-time updates to the frontend, such as progress updates during summarization or new notification alerts.\n*   **Server-Sent Events (SSE):** As an alternative to WebSockets, Server-Sent Events can be used for unidirectional real-time communication from the server to the client.\n*   **Event Bus:** An event bus is used to facilitate communication between different services. When a new content is uploaded, an event is published to the event bus, triggering the summarization service to process the content.\n*   **Notifications:** Real-time notifications are sent to users when their content has been summarized, or when there are important system updates. These notifications are delivered using WebSockets or SSE.\n\n## Authentication & Authorization with RBAC\n\nThe system employs a robust authentication and authorization mechanism based on Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) to ensure secure access to resources.\n\n*   **Authentication:** Users are authenticated using JWT (JSON Web Tokens). When a user logs in, the server generates a JWT containing the user's ID and roles. The client stores the JWT and includes it in the headers of subsequent requests. The server validates the JWT on each request to ensure that the user is authenticated.\n*   **Authorization:** RBAC is used to control access to resources based on the user's role. Each role is assigned a set of permissions, and users are assigned to roles. When a user attempts to access a resource, the system checks whether the user's role has the required permission.\n*   **Permissions:** Permissions are defined at a granular level to allow fine-grained control over access to resources. For example, there are separate permissions for creating users, editing content, and deleting summaries.\n*   **Middleware:** Middleware is used to enforce RBAC in the API layer. The middleware checks whether the user has the required permission to access the endpoint and returns an error if the user is not authorized.\n\n## Deployment & Scalability\n\nThe application is designed to be deployed on a cloud platform such as AWS, Azure, or Google Cloud. Containerization using Docker and orchestration using Kubernetes are used to ensure scalability and portability.\n\n*   **Containerization:** All services are packaged as Docker containers. This ensures that the application can be deployed consistently across different environments.\n*   **Orchestration:** Kubernetes is used to orchestrate the Docker containers. Kubernetes automates the deployment, scaling, and management of the containers.\n*   **Load Balancing:** A load balancer is used to distribute traffic across multiple instances of the application. This ensures that the application can handle a large volume of requests.\n*   **Auto-Scaling:** Auto-scaling is enabled to automatically scale the number of instances of the application based on traffic.\n*   **Database Scaling:** The database is configured to scale horizontally using techniques such as sharding and replication.\n*   **CDN:** A Content Delivery Network (CDN) is used to cache static assets such as images, CSS, and JavaScript files. This improves the performance of the application for users around the world.\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\nA comprehensive testing strategy is implemented to ensure the quality and reliability of the application. This includes unit tests, integration tests, and end-to-end tests.\n\n*   **Unit Tests:** Unit tests are written to test individual components and functions in isolation.\n*   **Integration Tests:** Integration tests are written to test the interaction between different components and services.\n*   **End-to-End Tests:** End-to-end tests are written to test the entire application from the user's perspective.\n*   **Regression Tests:** Regression tests are written to ensure that new features or bug fixes do not introduce new problems.\n*   **Performance Tests:** Performance tests are conducted to measure the performance of the application under different load conditions.\n*   **Security Tests:** Security tests are conducted to identify and address security vulnerabilities in the application.\n*   **Automated Testing:** Automated testing is used to run tests automatically on a continuous basis.\n*   **CI/CD:** Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) is used to automate the build, testing, and deployment process.\n\n## Security & Hardening\n\nSecurity is a top priority, and various measures are taken to protect the application from attacks and vulnerabilities.\n\n*   **Input Validation:** All user input is validated to prevent injection attacks such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS).\n*   **Authentication:** Strong authentication mechanisms are used to verify the identity of users.\n*   **Authorization:** RBAC is used to control access to resources based on user roles.\n*   **Encryption:** Sensitive data such as passwords and API keys are encrypted using strong encryption algorithms.\n*   **HTTPS:** HTTPS is used to encrypt all communication between the client and the server.\n*   **Rate Limiting:** Rate limiting is used to prevent denial-of-service (DoS) attacks.\n*   **Regular Security Audits:** Regular security audits are conducted to identify and address security vulnerabilities.\n*   **Dependency Management:** Dependencies are carefully managed to ensure that they are up-to-date and free of known vulnerabilities.\n*   **Penetration Testing:** Penetration testing is conducted to simulate real-world attacks and identify weaknesses in the security of the application.\n\n## Logging & Observability\n\nComprehensive logging and observability are implemented to monitor the health and performance of the application.\n\n*   **Centralized Logging:** All logs are collected in a centralized logging system such as Elasticsearch, Logstash, and Kibana (ELK stack).\n*   **Metrics:** Metrics are collected to monitor the performance of the application, such as CPU usage, memory usage, and request latency.\n*   **Tracing:** Tracing is used to track requests as they flow through the different services in the application.\n*   **Alerting:** Alerts are configured to notify administrators when there are problems with the application.\n*   **Dashboards:** Dashboards are created to visualize the logs, metrics, and traces.\n*   **Health Checks:** Health checks are implemented to monitor the health of the application and automatically restart failing services.\n*   **Audit Logging:** Audit logs are maintained to track all changes made to the system, such as user logins, content uploads, and model updates.\n\n## Features Overview\n\nThe AI Text Summarizer offers a comprehensive suite of features designed to enhance productivity and comprehension.\n\n1.  Text Summarization: Generates concise summaries of text documents.\n2.  Audio Summarization: Summarizes audio content through transcription and analysis.\n3.  Video Summarization: Extracts key information from video content.\n4.  Multi-Language Support: Supports summarization in multiple languages.\n5.  Customizable Summary Length: Allows users to specify the desired length of the summary.\n6.  Keyword Extraction: Identifies and highlights key keywords in the original content.\n7.  Source Citation: Automatically cites the original source of the information.\n8.  User Authentication: Secure user authentication and authorization.\n9.  Role-Based Access Control (RBAC): Granular access control based on user roles.\n10. Content Upload: Allows users to upload text, audio, and video files.\n11. URL Input: Enables summarization of content from web URLs.\n12. Copy-Paste Functionality: Allows users to directly paste text for summarization.\n13. Summary History: Maintains a history of all generated summaries.\n14. Model Selection: Allows users to choose the AI model used for summarization.\n15. Feedback Mechanism: Enables users to provide feedback on summary quality.\n16. Admin Dashboard: Comprehensive admin dashboard for managing users and content.\n17. User Management: Tools for managing user accounts and roles.\n18. Content Moderation: Features for moderating user-generated content.\n19. System Configuration: Settings for configuring system parameters.\n20. Logging and Monitoring: Comprehensive logging and monitoring capabilities.\n21. Real-time Updates: Real-time progress updates during summarization.\n22. Notification System: Notifications for successful summarization and system updates.\n23. Search Functionality: Search across contents and summaries.\n24. Tagging and Categorization: Allows users to tag and categorize content.\n25. Customizable Settings: Customizable user settings for language and preferences.\n26. Collaboration Features: Allows users to share and collaborate on summaries.\n27. API Access: Provides an API for integration with other applications.\n28. Support Resources: Access to FAQs and support documentation.\n29. Integration with Cloud Storage: Integration with cloud storage services (e.g., Google Drive, Dropbox).\n30. Analytics Dashboard: Provides analytics on usage and performance.\n31. Export Options: Ability to export summaries in various formats (e.g., TXT, PDF, DOCX).\n32. Sentiment Analysis: Provides sentiment analysis of the original content.\n33. Topic Modeling: Identifies and models the main topics covered in the content.\n34. Text-to-Speech: Converts summaries to speech.\n35. Automated Transcription: Automatically transcribes audio and video content.\n36. Browser Extension: A browser extension for quickly summarizing web pages.\n37. Mobile App Support: Native mobile apps for iOS and Android.\n38. Reporting: Generate reports on content summarized and model performance\n39. Version Control: Ability to revert and view previous versions of summaries\n40. Batch Summarization: Summarize multiple documents as a batch job\n\n## Feature Implementation Roadmap\n\nOkay, here's a feature implementation roadmap for the `ai-text-summarizer` project, focusing on the 5 most important features to get the project off the ground.\n\n### 1. Text Summarization\n**Description:** Allows users to paste or upload text and receive a concise summary generated by an AI model. This feature is the core of the application and the primary value proposition.\n\n**User Story:** As a student, I want to paste my research paper into the summarizer and get a shorter version that highlights the key points, so that I can quickly understand the main arguments and save time on my literature review.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n- Text input area on the frontend.\n- Backend integration with a text summarization AI model (e.g., using transformers library in Python with a model like BART or T5).\n- Error handling for invalid text input.\n- Display of the generated summary on the frontend.\n- Loading state indication while the AI model processes the input.\n- Character limit to the input text field.\n\n**Database Changes:**\n- `summaries` table:\n    - `id` (INT, PRIMARY KEY, AUTO_INCREMENT)\n    - `user_id` (INT, FOREIGN KEY referencing `users.id`, nullable)\n    - `original_text` (TEXT)\n    - `summary_text` (TEXT)\n    - `created_at` (TIMESTAMP)\n    - `updated_at` (TIMESTAMP)\n\n**Backend Components:**\n- **Models:**\n    - `Summary`: Represents a summary record in the database.\n- **Controllers:**\n    - `SummaryController`: Handles requests related to summary creation, retrieval, and deletion.\n- **Services:**\n    - `SummaryService`: Contains the business logic for generating summaries, interacting with the AI model, and saving summaries to the database.\n    - `AISummarizationService`: A service to interact with the chosen summarization AI model. This could potentially allow for swapping between different AI models in the future.\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n- `TextSummarizer.tsx`:  Main component with text input, summary output, and loading indicator.\n- `SummaryDisplay.tsx`: Reusable component to display a summary.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n- `POST /api/summaries`: Creates a new summary record.  Request body: `{ text: string }`.  Response: `{ id: number, summary: string }`.\n- `GET /api/summaries/:id`: Retrieves a summary by ID. Response: `{ id: number, originalText: string, summary: string }`.\n\n### 2. User Authentication\n**Description:** Allows users to create accounts, log in, and manage their profiles. This enables personalized experiences and the ability to save summaries.\n\n**User Story:** As a professional, I want to create an account so that I can save my summaries for later reference and track my research progress.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n- User registration form with email/username, password, and optional profile information.\n- Secure password storage using hashing.\n- Login and logout functionality.\n- Session management.\n- Profile management (ability to update profile information).\n\n**Database Changes:**\n- `users` table:\n    - `id` (INT, PRIMARY KEY, AUTO_INCREMENT)\n    - `username` (VARCHAR(255), UNIQUE, NOT NULL)\n    - `email` (VARCHAR(255), UNIQUE, NOT NULL)\n    - `password` (VARCHAR(255), NOT NULL) - Stored as a hash\n    - `created_at` (TIMESTAMP)\n    - `updated_at` (TIMESTAMP)\n\n**Backend Components:**\n- **Models:**\n    - `User`: Represents a user record in the database.\n- **Controllers:**\n    - `AuthController`: Handles registration, login, logout, and password reset requests.\n    - `UserController`: Handles user profile management.\n- **Services:**\n    - `AuthService`: Contains authentication logic, including password hashing and session management.\n    - `UserService`: Contains logic for managing user data.\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n- `Register.tsx`: User registration form.\n- `Login.tsx`: User login form.\n- `Profile.tsx`: User profile management page.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n- `POST /api/auth/register`: Registers a new user. Request body: `{ username: string, email: string, password: string }`.\n- `POST /api/auth/login`: Logs in an existing user. Request body: `{ email: string, password: string }`.  Response: `{ token: string }`.\n- `POST /api/auth/logout`: Logs out the current user.\n- `GET /api/users/me`: Gets the current user's profile information.  Requires authentication.\n- `PUT /api/users/me`: Updates the current user's profile information. Requires authentication.  Request body: `{ username?: string, email?: string }`.\n\n### 3. Summary History\n**Description:** Allows users to view, manage, and delete their previously generated summaries. This provides a personal archive of summarized content.\n\n**User Story:** As a researcher, I want to be able to view a history of all the summaries I've generated, so I can easily find and re-access previously summarized research papers.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n- Display a list of summaries with basic information (title, date, first few words of summary).\n- Ability to view the full summary.\n- Ability to delete summaries.\n- Pagination for long history lists.\n- Filtering/sorting options for summary list.\n\n**Database Changes:**\n- The `summaries` table from Feature 1 already supports this.  Consider adding a `title` field to the `summaries` table:\n    - `title` (VARCHAR(255), NULLABLE) - Optional title given to the summary by the user.\n\n**Backend Components:**\n- **Controllers:**\n    - `SummaryController`: (Expanded) Handles retrieval of summary lists for a specific user.\n- **Services:**\n    - `SummaryService`: (Expanded) Contains logic for retrieving paginated summary lists based on user ID.\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n- `SummaryHistory.tsx`: Displays the list of summaries with pagination, filtering, and delete options.\n- `SummaryItem.tsx`: Reusable component for displaying a single summary in the history list.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n- `GET /api/summaries`: Gets a list of summaries for the current user. Requires authentication.  Supports pagination parameters (e.g., `page`, `limit`).  Response: `[{ id: number, title: string, summary: string, createdAt: string }]`.\n- `DELETE /api/summaries/:id`: Deletes a summary by ID. Requires authentication.\n\n### 4. File Upload Summarization\n**Description:** Allows users to upload text files (e.g., .txt, .pdf, .docx) and receive a summary of the content. This supports larger documents and simplifies the input process.\n\n**User Story:** As a student, I want to be able to upload my lecture notes in a .txt file and have them automatically summarized, so that I don't have to manually copy and paste the text.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n- File upload component on the frontend.\n- Backend handling of file uploads.\n- Text extraction from uploaded files (.txt, .pdf, .docx).\n- Integration with the AI summarization model.\n- Error handling for invalid file types or corrupted files.\n- Maximum file size limit.\n\n**Database Changes:**\n- No new tables required. Existing `summaries` table is used.\n\n**Backend Components:**\n- **Controllers:**\n    - `SummaryController`: (Expanded) Handles requests for file uploads and summarization.\n- **Services:**\n    - `FileParsingService`: Parses uploaded files (txt, docx, pdf) and extracts the text content.\n    - `SummaryService`: (Expanded) Integrates with `FileParsingService` to summarize text from files.\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n- `FileUpload.tsx`: File upload component.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n- `POST /api/summaries/upload`: Uploads a file and creates a new summary record. Request body: `multipart/form-data` with a file named `file`. Response: `{ id: number, summary: string }`.\n\n### 5. User Feedback and Model Improvement\n**Description:** Allows users to provide feedback on the quality of the generated summaries. This feedback can be used to improve the AI model's performance over time.\n\n**User Story:** As a professional, I want to be able to provide feedback on the accuracy and relevance of the summaries, so that the AI model can be improved and provide even better summaries in the future.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\n- Feedback form on the frontend (e.g., thumbs up/thumbs down, star rating, text comment).\n- Backend storage of user feedback.\n- Mechanism for analyzing feedback and using it to retrain or fine-tune the AI model (this is a longer-term goal).\n\n**Database Changes:**\n- `summary_feedback` table:\n    - `id` (INT, PRIMARY KEY, AUTO_INCREMENT)\n    - `summary_id` (INT, FOREIGN KEY referencing `summaries.id`, NOT NULL)\n    - `user_id` (INT, FOREIGN KEY referencing `users.id`, NULLABLE)\n    - `rating` (ENUM('thumbs_up', 'thumbs_down', '1', '2', '3', '4', '5'), NULLABLE)\n    - `comment` (TEXT, NULLABLE)\n    - `created_at` (TIMESTAMP)\n\n**Backend Components:**\n- **Models:**\n    - `SummaryFeedback`: Represents a feedback record in the database.\n- **Controllers:**\n    - `FeedbackController`: Handles requests for submitting feedback on summaries.\n- **Services:**\n    - `FeedbackService`: Contains logic for saving feedback to the database.\n    - `ModelTrainingService` (future): Handles the process of retraining the AI model using user feedback.\n\n**Frontend Components:**\n- `FeedbackForm.tsx`: Displays the feedback form after a summary is generated.\n\n**API Endpoints:**\n- `POST /api/feedback`: Submits feedback on a summary. Request body: `{ summaryId: number, rating?: string, comment?: string }`. Requires authentication.\n",
